Let's get started.

19
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:30,628
Let's start by answering a fundamental question.

20
00:01:31,182 --> 00:01:32,900
What exactly are AI agents?

21
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:38,532
To understand this concept, we need to think about the difference between passive and active systems.

22
00:01:39,278 --> 00:01:46,148
Now, most traditional AI systems that you might be familiar with work like very sophisticated calculators.

23
00:01:46,446 --> 00:01:50,788
You give them an input, they process it using their training, and then they give you an output.

24
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:58,084
This is what we call a passive approach, because the system simply responds to what you provide without taking any independent action.

25
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:01,988
Now, AI agents, they work completely differently.

26
00:02:02,638 --> 00:02:06,372
So these are systems that actively engage with the world around them.

27
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:21,636
Instead of just waiting for someone to ask them a question, agents can observe their environment, make plans about what they want to accomplish, make decisions about how to achieve those goals, and then actually take those actions to make things happen.

28
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:34,340
So think of the difference between a calculator that waits for you to enter a number versus a robot that can see a messy room, decide that it needs to be cleaned, figure out how to exactly clean it, and then actually start cleaning.

29
00:02:35,182 --> 00:02:40,580
So the key characteristic that makes something an AI agent is the ability to perceive and also act.

30
00:02:40,878 --> 00:02:44,320
Agents can gather information about their surroundings through various means.

31
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:51,652
They can process that information to understand what's happening, and then they can use that understanding to influence their environment in meaningful ways.

32
00:02:52,046 --> 00:03:00,516
Now, this creates what we call an active loop, where the agent continuously observes things and then also acts based on what it actually learns.

33
00:03:01,038 --> 00:03:05,604
Now, a perfect example that most people can relate to is the self-driving car.

34
00:03:06,126 --> 00:03:11,200
So this technology represents one of the most advanced examples of AI agents in action.

35
00:03:11,502 --> 00:03:15,012
A self-driving car doesn't actually just sit there waiting for commands.

36
00:03:15,342 --> 00:03:20,720
Instead, it actively uses multiple types of sensors, including cameras that work like its eyes.

37
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:30,000
It has radar systems that can detect objects even in poor weather conditions, and also other sensing technologies to constantly gather information about everything around.

38
00:03:30,542 --> 00:03:43,652
So the car's computer systems process all of this incoming data to build a complete picture of the traffic situation, of the road conditions, pedestrians, and other vehicles, and also a bunch of different other factors that affect safe driving.

39
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:46,372
But information gathering is only half the story.

40
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:54,532
The self-driving car also has actuators, which are the mechanical systems that allow it to take physical actions in the real world.

41
00:03:54,990 --> 00:04:00,836
So these actuators control the staying wheel, they control the brakes, the accelerator, and other vehicle systems.

42
00:04:01,294 --> 00:04:14,756
So based on what the car sensors detect and also what its computer brain decides is the best course of action, it can actually steer around obstacles, slow down for traffic, speed up when it's safe, and then also navigate complex driving scenarios.

43
00:04:15,534 --> 00:04:20,708
Now what makes this even more impressive is that these systems learn and also improve over time.

44
00:04:21,198 --> 00:04:29,604
The car's cognitive modules, which are the AI components responsible for decision making, continuously analyze new patterns and situations that they encounter.

45
00:04:30,382 --> 00:04:38,576
So every time that the car faces a new driving scenario, it adds that experience to its knowledge, base, and then refines its driving skills accordingly.

46
00:04:38,816 --> 00:04:47,876
This means that the AI agents, like self-driving cars, become more capable and sophisticated as they gain more experience, much like how human drivers improve with practice.

47
00:04:48,622 --> 00:04:56,740
So this example illustrates the three core characteristics that define any AI agent: perception, decision making, and action.

48
00:04:57,262 --> 00:05:12,976
The agent must be able to sense and understand its environment, process that information to make intelligent decisions about what it needs to do next, and then also have the capability to actually implement those decisions by taking concrete actions that change something in the real world.

49
00:05:13,262 --> 00:05:20,708
Now that we understand what AI agents are in general, let's focus on a specific and particularly powerful type called generative AI agents.

50
00:05:21,614 --> 00:05:31,396
So these represent an exciting evolution of AI technology that combines the autonomous capabilities that we just discussed with the creative and reasoning powers of generative AI.

51
00:05:32,206 --> 00:05:44,772
So, to understand Gen AI agents, think of them as AI agents that have been enhanced with the ability to generate new content, ideas, and also solutions instead of just following pre-written scripts or OOLs.

52
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:50,372
So traditional AI systems, they often work like very sophisticated filing cabinets.

53
00:05:50,894 --> 00:05:58,980
They can find and retrieve information that was previously stored, but they do tend to struggle to create something entirely new.

54
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:15,428
Generative AI agents, on the other hand, can actually create novel and relevant responses, solutions and outputs that have never existed before, while still maintaining the perception, decision-making, and also to action capabilities that define an AI agent.

55
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:20,612
So what makes these systems particularly remarkable is their autonomy.

56
00:06:20,846 --> 00:06:35,044
So unlike traditional software that require detailed step-by-step programming for every possible situation, generative AI agents can independently handle complex scenarios by using the reasoning capabilities provided by large language models.

57
00:06:35,278 --> 00:06:50,756
So instead of a programmer having to anticipate every possible situation and write specific code for each one, these agents can encounter completely new problems and figure out solutions on their own by applying their underlying understanding of language, logic, and problem solving.

58
00:06:51,182 --> 00:06:55,876
The perception capabilities of generative AI agents extend far beyond simple data input.

59
00:06:56,366 --> 00:07:06,596
So these systems can work with text-based communications, visual information, audio, signals, and various other types of input to build comprehensive understanding of their environment.

60
00:07:07,598 --> 00:07:19,076
More importantly, they can interact naturally with other computer systems, databases, and even human users using conversational interfaces that feel intuitive and also human-like.

61
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:25,796
This means that you don't need to learn special commands or technical languages to communicate with these agents.

62
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:29,028
You can often just explain what you need in plain English.

63
00:07:29,518 --> 00:07:38,212
When it comes to taking action, generative AI agents excel at determining the best approach to accomplish their goals and then executing the necessary tasks to make it happen.

64
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:48,096
They do this by selecting from a toolkit of available functions, arranging those functions in the right sequence, and then calling upon various tools and resources that have been available to them.

65
00:07:48,366 --> 00:07:58,564
Think of this like having a highly skilled assistant who not only understands what you want accomplished, but also knows how to use all of the different tools and systems in your office to get the job done efficiently.

66
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:05,220
Now, perhaps most importantly, these agents are designed with goal-oriented behavior at their core.

67
00:08:05,678 --> 00:08:14,976
This means that they can take broad, complex objectives and automatically break them down into smaller, manageable steps that lead toward the desired outcome.

68
00:08:15,310 --> 00:08:22,372
For example, if you asked a generative AI agent to help plan a marketing campaign, it wouldn't just give you generic advice.

69
00:08:22,766 --> 00:08:39,428
Instead, it might research your industry, analyze your target audience, generate creative content ideas, suggest distribution channels, and even help you create timelines and budgets, all while working towards the overarching goal of building an effective marketing strategy.

70
00:08:40,142 --> 00:08:56,772
So the combination of these four characteristics autonomy, advanced perception, sophisticated action capabilities, and also goal-oriented behavior, creates AI systems that can work with minimal human supervision while adapting to new situations and requirements.

71
00:08:57,614 --> 00:09:07,876
So this represents a significant leap forward from earlier AI technologies and also opens up possibilities for automation and assistance that were previously impossible to achieve.

72
00:09:08,206 --> 00:09:15,044
Let's break down the technical architecture of generative AI agents into essential components to understand how these systems actually work.

73
00:09:15,566 --> 00:09:31,204
Now, at its core, a generative AI agent combines two fundamental capabilities, the think power of large language models, and the practical ability to execute tools, and also take actions in the real world, so the architecture follows a pretty straightforward but powerful pattern.

74
00:09:31,566 --> 00:09:38,852
When you give an agent a task, the system begins with a planning phase where the large language model serves as the brain of the operation.

75
00:09:39,438 --> 00:09:47,876
So this planning component analyzes your request and then formulates a series of actions, creates a roadmap to achieve the specific goal that you've outlined.

76
00:09:48,302 --> 00:09:57,008
Think of this like having a very intelligent project manager who can look at complex tasks and also immediately understand what steps need to be taken and also in what order.

77
00:09:57,248 --> 00:09:59,396
Also, what resources will be required.

78
00:10:00,014 --> 00:10:11,044
So during this planning phase, the large language model guides the agent through understanding exactly what you're asking for, and then also selecting the most appropriate tool from its available inventory.

79
00:10:11,438 --> 00:10:16,036
And finally, structuring a logical sequence of actions that'll lead to the desired outcome.

80
00:10:17,166 --> 00:10:29,444
So the sophistication here lies in the model's ability to reason through complex problems, consider multiple approaches, and then also choose the most effective strategy based on the specific circumstance of your request.

81
00:10:30,414 --> 00:10:35,088
Now, once the planning is complete, the system then moves into the action phase.

82
00:10:35,406 --> 00:10:39,328
So this is a part where it executes the tools that were selected during the planning phase.

83
00:10:39,566 --> 00:10:44,420
This is where the agent transitions from thinking about what to do to actually doing it comes in.

84
00:10:44,718 --> 00:10:50,852
The action capabilities can include a wide variety of functions depending on what tools have been made available to the agent.

85
00:10:51,438 --> 00:11:01,668
For example, read-only actions might involve retrieving documents from storage systems, searching through databases for specific information or gathering data from various sources.

86
00:11:02,158 --> 00:11:11,716
Write actions, on the other hand, allow the agent to modify its environment by sending emails, updating database records, creating new files, or making changes to existing systems.

87
00:11:12,942 --> 00:11:19,172
Now, what makes this architecture particularly powerful is the feedback loop between planning and action.

88
00:11:19,758 --> 00:11:24,100
The agent doesn't just execute a predetermined sequence of steps and then hope for the best.

89
00:11:24,718 --> 00:11:29,220
Instead, it evaluates the output from each tool and also the action that it takes.

90
00:11:29,582 --> 00:11:33,284
Then it uses that information to adapt its approach as needed.

91
00:11:33,902 --> 00:11:39,588
So if something doesn't work as expected, the agent can replan or escalate to alternative strategies.

92
00:11:39,950 --> 00:11:44,516
If an action succeeds, it can use the results to inform the next steps in the process.

93
00:11:45,486 --> 00:11:52,528
The tools themselves are the specific resources that extend the agent's capabilities beyond its own internal knowledge.

94
00:11:52,846 --> 00:12:07,568
So these might include application programming interfaces that connect to external software, systems, database connections that allow access to stored information, web services that provide real-time data, or specialized software functions that perform particular tasks.

95
00:12:07,918 --> 00:12:14,852
The key insight here is that these tools allow the agent to interact and also modify world beyond just generating text responses.

96
00:12:15,598 --> 00:12:25,568
This architecture creates a complete cycle where the agent receives input in the form of a task, processes that input through intelligent planning, that's powered by the large language models.

97
00:12:25,774 --> 00:12:33,092
It can then take concrete actions using the available tools and then produce meaningful outputs that accomplish the original objective.

98
00:12:33,710 --> 00:12:45,988
The result is a system that can understand natural language requests, reason about complex problems, and also execute solutions that involve multiple steps and interaction, various systems and databases.

99
00:12:46,510 --> 00:12:50,928
The beauty of this approach is its flexibility and extensibility.

100
00:12:51,310 --> 00:12:58,212
So by changing the tools available to an agent, you can dramatically alter its capabilities without modifying the underlying intelligence.

101
00:12:58,734 --> 00:13:11,808
This means that the same planning and reasoning system can be adapted for customer service, for data analysis, for content creation, or countless other applications simply by providing it with the appropriate tools for each domain.

102
00:13:12,014 --> 00:13:17,572
Now, we arrive at the most sophisticated level of our AI technology progression, generative AI agents.

103
00:13:18,094 --> 00:13:27,236
Now, these represent the current pinnacle of autonomous AI systems and also demonstrate capabilities that truly distinguish them from all of the other technologies that we've discussed.

104
00:13:28,046 --> 00:13:38,116
So, understanding agents is crucial because they represent the direction that AI automation is heading and also offer solutions to problems that simpler AI systems can't effectively address.

105
00:13:39,182 --> 00:13:49,572
So, generative AI agents are fundamentally different from workflows, assistance, and basic language models because they operate with true autonomy in how they approach complex tasks.

106
00:13:50,350 --> 00:14:06,980
So instead of following some predetermined steps or responding to specific inputs, agents can autonomously break down complex, multifaceted tasks into whatever subtasks they determine are necessary and then carry out those subtasks using the tools and resources that are available to them.

107
00:14:07,598 --> 00:14:17,572
Now, this represents a significant leap from the prescriptive nature of workflows to a dynamic, adaptive approach that can handle unpredictable situations.

108
00:14:18,030 --> 00:14:22,244
Now, what makes agents truly remarkable is their goal-oriented nature.

109
00:14:22,926 --> 00:14:28,388
When you give an agent a high-level objective, it doesn't just execute a predetermined sequence of actions.

110
00:14:28,814 --> 00:14:37,444
Instead, it analyzes the goal, considers the current context, and also the available resources, and then it develops its own strategy for achievement.

111
00:14:37,838 --> 00:14:45,572
The agent then dynamically adjusts its strategy as it encounters new information, obstacles, or opportunities during the execution process.

112
00:14:47,054 --> 00:14:51,812
Now, this is similar to how a skilled human employee might approach a complex project.

113
00:14:52,430 --> 00:14:59,652
They understand the end goal, and then they figure out the best path to get there, adapting their approach as the circumstances change.

114
00:15:00,558 --> 00:15:05,796
The autonomous breakdown capability is probably the most impressive aspect of agents.

115
00:15:06,670 --> 00:15:26,980
Unlike workflows where you must predefine every step, agents can actually encounter a complex task that they've never seen before, and can independently determine what subtasks are required, in what order they should be executed, and also how the results of each subtask should inform subsequent actions.

116
00:15:27,694 --> 00:15:36,708
Now, this means that agents can handle novel situations and also unexpected complications without requiring human intervention or reprogramming.

117
00:15:36,974 --> 00:15:40,336
Agents are also distinguished by their sophisticated tool usage.

118
00:15:40,750 --> 00:15:50,948
While workflows might use predetermined tools at specific steps, agents can dynamically select from their available toolkit based on what they determine is the most appropriate given the current situation.

119
00:15:51,406 --> 00:16:05,412
They can combine tools in creative ways, use the output from one tool into another to inform the choice of subsequent tools, and even recognize when their current toolkit is insufficient and request additional resources or escalate to human assistance.

120
00:16:06,990 --> 00:16:14,404
The goal-oriented systems approach means that the agents can maintain focus on the ultimate objective throughout their operation.

121
00:16:14,926 --> 00:16:24,708
They don't just execute tasks mechanically, they continuously evaluate whether their current actions are moving them closer to the goal and adjust their strategy accordingly.

122
00:16:25,006 --> 00:16:32,132
This creates a level of purposeful behavior that more closely resembles human problem solving than traditional automation.

123
00:16:33,198 --> 00:16:42,084
Understanding when to deploy generative AI agents requires recognizing scenarios where their unique capabilities provide clear advantages over simpler AI technologies.

124
00:16:42,702 --> 00:16:49,316
Agents are not always the best solution, but in certain situations they represent the only practical approach to automation.

125
00:16:49,774 --> 00:16:56,736
Agents are ideal for situations where it's difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps needed to accomplish a goal.

126
00:16:57,038 --> 00:17:05,028
Unlike workflows, where you can map out the process in advance, some tasks are inherently unpredictable in their complexity and requirements.

127
00:17:05,646 --> 00:17:16,644
For example, investigating a complex customer complaint might require anywhere from three simple steps to dozens of intricate actions depending on what initial investigation reveals.

128
00:17:17,358 --> 00:17:26,052
An agent can start with the complaint and dynamically determine what research, analysis, and resolution steps are needed based on the specific circumstances.

129
00:17:26,830 --> 00:17:31,268
Similarly, agents excel when a fixed path can't be hard-coded in advance.

130
00:17:31,726 --> 00:17:37,956
Some business processes are simply too variable and context dependent to be captured in predetermined workflows.

131
00:17:38,606 --> 00:17:49,764
Market research projects, competitive analysis, or strategic planning initiatives often require adaptive approaches where the next step depends entirely on what was discovered in the previous step.

132
00:17:50,350 --> 00:17:57,796
Agents can handle this kind of dynamic decision making because they can evaluate new information and also adjust their approach in real time.

133
00:17:58,350 --> 00:18:08,420
Goal-based agents particularly excel in complex decision-making environments where they need to evaluate potential actions and choose optimal strategies based on the current conditions.

134
00:18:09,294 --> 00:18:13,216
Financial trading represents excellent examples of this capability.

135
00:18:13,486 --> 00:18:22,020
The financial markets are constantly changing, with new information that's affecting prices, trading volumes, and also market sentiment every single second.

136
00:18:22,510 --> 00:18:35,204
The trading agent must continuously analyze market conditions, evaluate the multiple potential trading strategies, assess the risk levels, and also execute trades based on complex criteria that can change throughout the entire day.

137
00:18:36,174 --> 00:18:41,476
In financial trading scenarios, agents demonstrate their full range of capabilities.

138
00:18:41,838 --> 00:19:00,420
They can monitor multiple data streams simultaneously, analyze patterns and trends that might not be apparent to human traders, they can evaluate potential trades against risk management criteria, they can execute transactions when conditions are optimal, and they can also continuously adjust their strategies based on market performance and changing conditions.

139
00:19:01,262 --> 00:19:09,540
This level of sophistication, real-time decision making, simply can't be achieved with predetermined workflows or just simple assistance.

140
00:19:10,094 --> 00:19:20,196
What makes agents particularly valuable in these scenarios is their ability to handle uncertainty and complexity while maintaining focus on strategic objectives.

141
00:19:21,294 --> 00:19:29,956
They can work with incomplete information, make reasonable assumptions when necessary, and also adapt their approach as new information becomes available.

142
00:19:30,350 --> 00:19:43,460
This mirrors how experienced human professionals handle complex, ambiguous situations, but with the advantage of being able to process vast amounts of information simultaneously and also execute actions with perfect consistency.

143
00:19:44,270 --> 00:19:54,276
The key insight here is that agents are designed for situations that require genuine intelligence and adaptability instead of just the automation of known processes.

144
00:19:55,150 --> 00:20:06,564
When you need a system that can think through problems, that can evaluate options, and also make strategic decisions in dynamic environments, agents provide capabilities that no other AI technologies can match.

145
00:20:07,086 --> 00:20:23,716
However, this sophistication comes with the increased complexity in implementation and management, which is why agents are best suited for scenarios where their unique capabilities are truly necessary, instead of just being deployed as a default solution for all automation needs.

146
00:20:24,110 --> 00:20:32,324
Continuing our exploration of AI agents, this slide delves into the fundamental building blocks that enable their autonomous and goal-oriented behavior.

147
00:20:32,846 --> 00:20:40,964
As we've discussed, AI agents are not simply passive systems, they actively engage with their environment to achieve the defined objectives.

148
00:20:41,870 --> 00:20:49,796
So this slide illustrates that this active engagement and goal attainment are powered by a set of distinct functional components working in concert.

149
00:20:50,414 --> 00:21:00,132
The central idea presented here is that an AI agent can be conceptually broken down into four key capabilities: planning, reasoning, execution, and memory.

150
00:21:00,814 --> 00:21:08,548
Think of these as the essential cognitive and action-oriented functions that allow an agent to perceive, think, decide, and also act effectively.

151
00:21:09,102 --> 00:21:12,064
Visually, this slide also presents a hierarchical diagram.

152
00:21:12,238 --> 00:21:15,812
At the top, we have a central node labeled as agents.

153
00:21:16,270 --> 00:21:18,756
This signifies the complete AI agent system.

154
00:21:19,182 --> 00:21:23,332
Now, branching out from the central node are four primary components arranged horizontally.

155
00:21:23,534 --> 00:21:27,812
Here we have planning, reasoning, execution, and memory.

156
00:21:28,238 --> 00:21:31,140
Let's examine each of these core components in detail.

157
00:21:32,046 --> 00:21:33,348
First, we have planning.

158
00:21:33,742 --> 00:21:46,404
Now, this component embodies the agent's capability to set goals, decompose complex objectives into smaller, manageable subtasks, and also devise strategic sequences of actions to ultimately achieve those objectives.

159
00:21:46,830 --> 00:21:55,844
Without a robust planning module, an agent would be reactive instead of being proactive, unable to chart a course towards a desired outcome.

160
00:21:56,846 --> 00:22:01,604
Consider a customer service agent that's tasked with resolving a complex billing inquiry.

161
00:22:02,126 --> 00:22:10,884
The planning component would enable it to first understand the overarching goal, which is to resolve the customer's billing issue, and also ensure the customer's satisfaction.

162
00:22:11,374 --> 00:22:25,444
It would then break this down into subtasks, such as verifying the customer's identity, retrieving their billing history, identifying the discrepancy, and also determining the appropriate resolution according to the company policy.

163
00:22:26,062 --> 00:22:28,612
Then it would formulate a response to the customer.

164
00:22:29,166 --> 00:22:33,348
Furthermore, planning also involves devising a sequence for these actions.

165
00:22:33,870 --> 00:22:38,852
For instance, it's logical to verify identity before you try to access sensitive billing information.

166
00:22:39,310 --> 00:22:45,380
Now, this ability to strategize and also create a roadmap is fundamental to intelligent agent behavior.

167
00:22:46,126 --> 00:22:47,556
Next up, we have reasoning.

168
00:22:47,854 --> 00:22:52,068
This is a crucial component that deals with the agent's ability to process information.

169
00:22:52,526 --> 00:23:02,724
It also deals with understanding the context in which it operates, make logical inferences based on the available data, and then ultimately formulate some decisions.

170
00:23:03,054 --> 00:23:07,264
Now, reasoning allows the agent to go beyond simply following pre-programmed steps.

171
00:23:07,502 --> 00:23:12,944
It enables it to analyze situations, draw conclusions, and also adapt its behavior accordingly.

172
00:23:13,230 --> 00:23:15,012
Imagine a financial trading agent.

173
00:23:15,310 --> 00:23:25,924
It needs to process vast amounts of market data, understand the current economic context, infer potential future price movements, and also make decisions about when to buy or to sell assets.

174
00:23:26,414 --> 00:23:34,468
The reasoning component allows it to analyze trends, correlate different pieces of information, and also make informed decisions under uncertainty.

175
00:23:35,054 --> 00:23:48,580
For example, if the agent observes a combination of rising interest rates and also declining consumer confidence, its reasoning module might infer a potential downturn in the market and also adjust its trading strategy to mitigate risk.

176
00:23:49,390 --> 00:23:51,744
The third core component is execution.

177
00:23:52,046 --> 00:23:58,628
This is where the agent translates its plans and also its decisions into tangible actions within its environment.

178
00:23:59,822 --> 00:24:07,588
So, execution encompasses the agent's ability to interact with the world and carry out the planned sequence of steps.

179
00:24:07,950 --> 00:24:11,104
It could often be through the use of various tools.

180
00:24:11,438 --> 00:24:20,036
These tools could be APIs to access your data, software applications to perform specific tasks, or even physical actuators in the robotic systems.

181
00:24:20,910 --> 00:24:23,012
Think back to our customer service agent.

182
00:24:23,342 --> 00:24:46,020
Once the planning and reasoning modules have determined the necessary steps to resolve a billing issue, perhaps issuing a refund and updating the customer's account, the execution component would involve using the appropriate tools, such as the customer relationship management or the CRM system, or a financial transaction API to actually initiate the refund and update the account details.

183
00:24:47,022 --> 00:24:58,820
Similarly, in our financial trading agent example, the execution component would involve connecting to trading platforms and executing buying or sell orders based on the decisions that are made by the reasoning module.

184
00:24:59,406 --> 00:25:04,196
Effective execution ensures that the agent's intentions are translated into real-world impact.

185
00:25:05,358 --> 00:25:07,344
Finally, we also have memory.

186
00:25:07,534 --> 00:25:16,004
This component provides the agent with the ability to store and also retrieve past experiences, accumulated knowledge, and also the relevant context.

187
00:25:16,750 --> 00:25:19,904
So, memory is crucial for the agent to learn over time.

188
00:25:20,174 --> 00:25:27,108
It is also crucial for it to adapt to changing circumstances and make more informed decisions based on its history.

189
00:25:27,598 --> 00:25:36,740
Without memory, an agent would essentially be starting fresh with every new task, lacking the ability to leverage past successes or failures.

190
00:25:37,326 --> 00:25:40,996
Consider a personalized recommendation agent on an e-commerce platform.

191
00:25:41,966 --> 00:25:50,148
This memory component would store information about a user's past purchases, their browsing history, preferences, and interactions within the platform.

192
00:25:50,702 --> 00:26:02,212
Now, when the user returns to the site, the agent can retrieve this information from its memory and then use it to reason about what products the user might be interested in, thereby generating more relevant and effective recommendations.

193
00:26:03,022 --> 00:26:13,264
For instance, if the memory indicates that a user frequently purchases coffee beans and also related accessories, the agent can proactively suggest new coffee blends or brewing equipment.

194
00:26:13,486 --> 00:26:22,052
Now, this ability to retain and also utilize past information enhances the agent's effectiveness and also provides a more personalized and effective experience.

195
00:26:22,638 --> 00:26:32,036
In summary, planning, reasoning, execution, and memory components are the foundational elements that enable AI agents to operate autonomously and also achieve their goals.

196
00:26:32,430 --> 00:26:38,404
The interplay between these components is what defines intelligence and also the capabilities of the AI agent.

197
00:26:38,830 --> 00:26:45,252
As we continue, we will see how these core components are instantiated in different AI agent architectures and also design patterns.

198
00:27:03,886 --> 00:27:08,772
In this slide, we delve into the current trends observed in the realm of generative AI agents.

199
00:27:09,136 --> 00:27:22,916
So this slide presents findings from a survey specifically addressing the perceived suitability of agents for various tasks and the primary limitations hindering their wider adoption in production environments.

200
00:27:23,182 --> 00:27:33,444
The data visualized in these charts offer valuable insights into the present state and also the near-term challenges in the deployment of generative AI agents.

201
00:27:35,022 --> 00:27:43,936
Looking at the first bar chart on the left, the question posed here is that, in your opinion, which tasks are agents best suited to perform today?

202
00:27:44,110 --> 00:27:52,336
The responses highlight the current strengths and also the perceived applicability of these intelligent systems across a spectrum of potential uses.

203
