This garners close to 58.2% of the responses.

205
00:28:01,296 --> 00:28:10,788
This suggests a strong belief that generative AI agents excel at sifting through vast amounts of information, identifying key insights, and producing concise summaries.

206
00:28:11,694 --> 00:28:18,176
Imagine a business scenario where an analyst needs to understand the competitive landscape for a new product.

207
00:28:18,350 --> 00:28:35,296
In this case, a generative AI agent could be tasked with analyzing market research reports, competitor websites, and industry news articles, ultimately delivering a comprehensive yet also a succinct summary of the key trends and competitive advantages.

208
00:28:35,536 --> 00:28:45,616
This capability significantly reduces the time and effort required for manual research, allowing human experts to focus on higher-level strategic decision making.

209
00:28:45,776 --> 00:28:50,148
Falling closely behind is personal assistance or productivity tasks.

210
00:28:50,496 --> 00:28:57,124
This is with 53.5% of respondents, indicating that this is a strong suit of today's agents.

211
00:28:57,326 --> 00:29:03,108
This encompasses a wide range of applications that are aimed at enhancing individual or even team productivity.

212
00:29:04,174 --> 00:29:15,376
Now, examples here could include managing schedules, drafting emails, setting reminders, organizing documents, and even automating routine workflows.

213
00:29:15,536 --> 00:29:20,932
Consider a sales professional who spends a significant amount of time following up with leads.

214
00:29:21,136 --> 00:29:35,056
A generative AI agent could be employed to track lead engagement, send personalized follow-up emails based on predefined criteria, and schedule meetings, thereby freeing up the salesperson's time to focus on building relationships and closing deals.

215
00:29:35,216 --> 00:29:45,136
The perceived strength in this area underscores the potential of AI agents to become integral tools for daily task management and efficiency enhancement.

216
00:29:45,376 --> 00:29:47,940
The next category is customer service.

217
00:29:48,176 --> 00:29:52,196
This has about 45.8% of the responses.

218
00:29:52,430 --> 00:30:00,416
This highlights the growing recognition of generative AI agents in transforming how businesses interact with their customers.

219
00:30:00,656 --> 00:30:09,856
Agents can be deployed to handle common customer inquiries, provide product information, troubleshoot issues, and even process basic transactions.

220
00:30:10,126 --> 00:30:17,216
Envision an e-commerce platform where customers frequently ask about shipping times and return policies.

221
00:30:17,454 --> 00:30:30,596
In a scenario like that, a generative AI agent could be implemented as a first line of support, capable of instantly answering these common questions based on the available information that's there in the knowledge base.

222
00:30:30,816 --> 00:30:41,776
This not only provides immediate assistance to customers, but also reduces the workload on human customer service agents, allowing them to focus on more complex or sensitive issues.

223
00:30:41,998 --> 00:30:48,416
Code generation is also another area where generative AI agents are seen as increasingly capable.

224
00:30:48,656 --> 00:30:53,616
This accounts for about 35.5% of the respondents selecting this option.

225
00:30:53,776 --> 00:31:07,056
This suggests that these agents are proving useful in assisting developers with writing code, generating boilerplate code, suggesting code improvements, and even translating code between different programming languages.

226
00:31:07,296 --> 00:31:10,736
Consider a software development team working on a new feature.

227
00:31:10,896 --> 00:31:20,256
A generative AI agent could be used to automatically generate the basic structure of a function or a class based on a high level description.

228
00:31:20,672 --> 00:31:32,192
Or even to suggest more efficient ways to implement a particular algorithm, this can significantly accelerate the development process and also potentially reduce the incidence of common coding errors.

229
00:31:32,352 --> 00:31:37,060
Data transformation and enrichment was selected by 33.8% of the respondents.

230
00:31:37,326 --> 00:31:45,476
This indicates a belief in the ability of generative AI agents to manipulate, clean, and also to augment data in meaningful ways.

231
00:31:45,712 --> 00:31:58,912
This could involve tasks such as converting data between different formats, identifying and also correcting inconsistencies, or maybe enriching datasets with information from external sources.

232
00:31:59,152 --> 00:32:02,832
Imagine a marketing team analyzing customer data from various channels.

233
00:32:02,992 --> 00:32:19,392
A generative AI agent could be used to automatically consolidate this data, cleaning it by removing duplicates and errors, and then also enriching it with demographic information from external databases, providing a more comprehensive view of their customer base for targeted campaigns.

234
00:32:19,662 --> 00:32:24,112
Lower on the list, interactive gameplay, storytelling, or companionship.

235
00:32:24,352 --> 00:32:26,432
This received about 19.6%.

236
00:32:26,912 --> 00:32:36,228
While perhaps less immediately business critical, this highlights the potential of generative AI agents in more creative and also interactive domains.

237
00:32:36,432 --> 00:32:46,340
This could involve creating dynamic narratives in games, generating personalized stories for entertainment, or even providing a conversational companion for users.

238
00:32:46,862 --> 00:32:58,832
Finally, the other category accounts for a small 2.9% of the responses, suggesting that the predefined options likely captured the majority of the perceived current strengths.

239
00:32:59,072 --> 00:33:07,712
Turning our attention to the bar chart on the right, the question posed over here is what is your biggest limitation of putting more agents in production?

240
00:33:07,950 --> 00:33:19,364
This chart reveals the key hurdles that organizations face when trying to move generative AI agents from experimental stages to widespread deployment in real-world applications.

241
00:33:19,566 --> 00:33:26,032
As we can see over here, the most significant limitation that's identified is performance quality.

242
00:33:26,192 --> 00:33:30,020
This is cited by a substantial 41% of respondents.

243
00:33:30,352 --> 00:33:38,032
This likely encompasses concerns around the accuracy, reliability, and consistency of the agent's outputs and actions.

244
00:33:38,222 --> 00:33:45,072
In a production environment, it is crucial that AI agents perform their tasks correctly and also predictably.

245
00:33:45,232 --> 00:33:56,592
For instance, in a customer service setting, an agent providing inaccurate information or failing to resolve an issue could lead to customer dissatisfaction and reputation damage.

246
00:33:56,846 --> 00:34:06,980
Addressing performance quality requires rigorous testing, robust error handling mechanisms, and continuous monitoring and improvement of the underlying models and agent architectures.

247
00:34:08,430 --> 00:34:12,352
Cost, as we see over here, is the next major limitation.

248
00:34:12,512 --> 00:34:25,152
According to 18.4% of the responses, deploying and maintaining generative AI agents can involve significant costs related to infrastructure, model training, API usage, and specialized personnel.

249
00:34:25,392 --> 00:34:34,992
Organizations need to carefully evaluate the return on investment and ensure that the benefits of using AI agents outweigh the associated expenses.

250
00:34:35,232 --> 00:34:49,924
For example, the computational resources required to run large language models, which often form the core of generative AI agents, can be quite substantial, and this can lead to significant operational costs.

251
00:34:50,192 --> 00:34:54,912
Tied with cost at 18.4% are safety concerns.

252
00:34:55,152 --> 00:35:07,140
As AI agents become more autonomous and interact with real-world systems, ensuring their safety and preventing unintended or harmful consequences is also going to be paramount.

253
00:35:07,312 --> 00:35:14,592
This includes concerns about bias in the model, the potential for misuse, and the need for robust control mechanisms.

254
00:35:14,832 --> 00:35:18,592
Consider an AI agent that is tasked with making financial or trading decisions.

255
00:35:18,830 --> 00:35:24,512
A flaw in its design or training could lead to significant financial losses or even market manipulation.

256
00:35:24,686 --> 00:35:36,484
Addressing safety concerns requires careful consideration of ethical implications, through thorough testing for potential vulnerabilities, and the implementation of appropriate safeguards.

257
00:35:36,672 --> 00:35:42,192
Latency is also another important limitation identified by 15.1% of respondents.

258
00:35:42,352 --> 00:35:49,872
In many real world applications, the speed at which an AI agent can respond or complete a task is gonna be critical.

259
00:35:50,094 --> 00:35:55,396
High latency can negatively impact user experience and also the overall effectiveness of the agent.

260
00:35:56,174 --> 00:36:05,616
For example, maybe in something like a conversational AI application, long delays in responses can lead to frustration and also a perception of poor performance.

261
00:36:05,838 --> 00:36:12,416
Optimizing the efficiency of the underlying models and also the overall system architecture is crucial for minimizing latency.

262
00:36:12,590 --> 00:36:30,336
Finally, the other category accounts for 7% of the reported limitations, suggesting that, yes, while the main challenges are captured by the predefined options, there might be other specific concerns depending on the particular use case and also deployment environment.

263
00:36:30,606 --> 00:36:44,096
Continuing our discussion on the landscape of generative AI agents, let's now delve into the trends that we are observing, specifically focusing on the dichotomy of the immense promise and also the practical hurdles we currently face.

264
00:36:44,256 --> 00:36:51,216
Now, this perspective is drawn from the MLOP's community survey conducted in 2025.

265
00:36:51,502 --> 00:36:56,976
Firstly, the adoption of generative AI agents is currently in its early stages.

266
00:36:57,216 --> 00:37:11,296
While there is significant excitement and a large number of individuals and organizations that are actively building and also experimenting with these systems, full production deployment remains relatively uncommon.

267
00:37:11,456 --> 00:37:18,656
This indicates a vibrant research and development landscape where the potential is being explored and also validated.

268
00:37:18,926 --> 00:37:23,856
But the transition to widespread or mission-critical applications is still underway.

269
00:37:24,046 --> 00:37:36,176
We are seeing a lot of proof-of-concept projects and initial implementations in controlled environments, but the robustness, reliability, and scalability required for widespread adoption are still being refined.

270
00:37:36,336 --> 00:37:47,856
This early stage allows for innovation and the exploration of diverse architectures and use cases, but it also means that best practices and mature development workflows are still evolving.

271
00:37:48,016 --> 00:37:51,536
The adoption of these agents is driven by several key factors.

272
00:37:51,790 --> 00:38:01,696
A primary driver is the potential for automating tasks, which can lead to significant efficiency gains and cost reductions across various industries.

273
00:38:01,870 --> 00:38:07,616
For example, in customer service, generative AI agents can automate the handling of routine inquiries.

274
00:38:07,822 --> 00:38:12,096
It helps to free up human agents to focus on more complex issues.

275
00:38:12,270 --> 00:38:18,596
Another crucial driver is the ability to increase efficiency in existing products and workflows.

276
00:38:19,342 --> 00:38:29,764
Imagine a content creation platform where an AI agent assists writers by generating initial drafts or perhaps suggesting improvements, thereby speeding up the content production process.

277
00:38:29,936 --> 00:38:36,096
Furthermore, these agents are enabling entirely new capabilities that were previously infeasible.

278
00:38:36,256 --> 00:38:47,856
For instance, in scientific research, AI agents can analyze vast data sets and propose novel hypotheses or experiments, accelerating the pace of discovery.

279
00:38:48,096 --> 00:38:56,016
These drivers highlight the compelling value proposition that generative AI agents offer, fueling the current interest and experimentation.

280
00:38:56,238 --> 00:39:02,916
Despite the promising outlook, there are significant challenges that persist in the development and deployment of generative AI agents.

281
00:39:03,182 --> 00:39:06,496
Reliability and consistency issues are major concerns.

282
00:39:06,702 --> 00:39:16,976
Unlike traditional software systems, the output of generative models can be unpredictable, and ensuring consistent performance across different inputs over time is a complex problem.

283
00:39:17,216 --> 00:39:20,816
High operational costs present another substantial barrier.

284
00:39:20,976 --> 00:39:30,896
Running large language models and the associated infrastructure can be expensive, particularly for complex agent workflows that require significant computational resources.

285
00:39:31,136 --> 00:39:34,532
Concerns around safety and control are also paramount.

286
00:39:34,736 --> 00:39:46,256
Ensuring that these autonomous systems behave ethically and also in accordance with intended guidelines is crucial, especially as they become more integrated into real world applications.

287
00:39:46,478 --> 00:39:49,776
Difficulties in evaluation pose a significant hurdle.

288
00:39:50,160 --> 00:40:03,760
Quantifying the performance and also the effectiveness of AI agents, particularly in tasks that involve creativity or subjective judgment, is challenging and also requires the development of new evaluation metrics and methodologies.

289
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:10,640
Finally, the overall complexity of development and tool integration adds to the practical challenges.

290
00:40:10,894 --> 00:40:21,520
Building sophisticated AI agents often involves integrating various components such as planning modules, memory systems, and tool execution capabilities, which can be technically demanding.

291
00:40:21,760 --> 00:40:30,080
The development of generative AI agents heavily depends on frameworks and libraries such as Langchain and also Llama Index to manage complexity.

292
00:40:30,240 --> 00:40:35,360
This tooling reliance is both a strength and also a potential vulnerability.

293
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:43,972
These frameworks provide valuable abstractions and functionalities that accelerate development and also simplify the integration of various components.

294
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:52,000
However, it also means that developers are somewhat tied to the capabilities and limitations of these underlying tools.

295
00:40:52,206 --> 00:41:02,240
The rapid evolution of this field also implies that these tools are constantly being updated, requiring developers to stay abreast of the latest changes and also potentially refactor their code.

296
00:41:02,414 --> 00:41:12,100
While these frameworks are essential for the current state of development, the field may benefit from greater standardization and interoperability in the long run.

297
00:41:12,462 --> 00:41:17,892
Finally, there is no universal agreement on what constitutes an agent.

298
00:41:18,080 --> 00:41:24,480
This lack of a clear and consistent definition leads to diversity in approaches and architectures.

299
00:41:24,720 --> 00:41:34,880
Different researchers and practitioners may have varying conceptualizations of agency, autonomy, and the essential components that define an AI agent.

300
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:45,040
This variability can make it quite challenging to compare different systems, share best practices, and also establish common benchmarks for evaluation.

301
00:41:45,230 --> 00:41:58,628
As the field matures, it is likely that a more unified understanding of what constitutes an AI agent will emerge, which will help to solidify the theoretical foundations and also facilitate more consistent development practices.

302
00:41:59,342 --> 00:42:22,436
Overall, this slide underscores that while all of the potential of generative AI agents is significant and the drivers for their adoption are strong, there are still substantial practical challenges related to reliability, cost, safety, evaluation, tooling, and even fundamental definitions that need to be addressed as the field progresses.

303
00:43:01,110 --> 00:43:10,750
To understand how generative AI agents fit into the broader landscape of AI technologies, we need to examine the evolutionary progression that has led us to where we are at currently.

304
00:43:10,990 --> 00:43:14,610
The foundation of this progression starts with large language models.

305
00:43:14,830 --> 00:43:24,800
These models excel at activities like summarizing lengthy documents, generating human-like text, translating between different languages, and also creating various types of written content.

306
00:43:25,038 --> 00:43:32,640
To provide coding assistance, large language models serve as foundational building blocks for more complex applications that involve reasoning and problem solving.

307
00:43:32,846 --> 00:43:38,560
They can understand programming concepts, analyze code structures, and also generate solutions to technical problems.

308
00:43:38,720 --> 00:43:42,240
However, at this stage, the interaction is still relatively simple.

309
00:43:42,446 --> 00:43:47,840
You ask a question or represent a problem, and then the model provides a response based on its training.

310
00:43:48,046 --> 00:43:54,480
Building upon this foundation, we encounter generative AI assistance, which represents the next step in complexity and capability.

311
00:43:54,640 --> 00:44:04,228
These systems take the language understanding capabilities of large models and enhance them with interactive features that make them more useful for ongoing conversations and support tasks.

312
00:44:04,686 --> 00:44:15,200
Now, assistants can maintain a history of previous interactions, which allow them to provide more contextually relevant responses and build upon earlier parts of your conversation.

313
00:44:15,406 --> 00:44:30,052
Generative AI assistants are particularly useful for customer support scenarios and also service automation because they can handle back and forth conversations, remember what was discussed earlier, and also leverage external data sources to provide more comprehensive assistance.

314
00:44:30,478 --> 00:44:40,880
These systems can access company databases, policy documents, and other information repositories to give you answers that are specifically relevant to your organization instead of just general knowledge.

315
00:44:41,040 --> 00:44:49,280
They can also integrate with existing systems using RAG, which means that they can pull in real-time information to supplement their responses.

316
00:44:49,518 --> 00:44:57,040
The next evolutionary step brings us to generative AI workflows, which break down complex tasks into manageable sequential processes.

317
00:44:57,262 --> 00:45:07,600
Instead of handling everything in a single interaction, workflows organize work into a fixed sequence of steps, where each step is executed by a language model call and possibly additional tools.

318
00:45:07,854 --> 00:45:14,240
This approach is ideal for situations where you have a clearly defined task that can be easily decomposed into smaller subtasks.

319
00:45:14,414 --> 00:45:30,320
For example, generating marketing copy might involve separate steps for researching the target audience, analyzing competitor messaging, creating initial content drafts, refining messaging for brand consistency, and also formatting the final output.

320
00:45:30,480 --> 00:45:35,200
Each of these steps can be handled systematically, with the output from one feeding into the next.

321
00:45:35,374 --> 00:45:47,760
This routing and parallelization approach can significantly increase the effectiveness of independent subtasks, especially for specialized processes like customer service inquiries, where you can predict the general flow of the resolution steps.

322
00:45:48,014 --> 00:45:51,440
Finally, we arrive at generative AI agents.

323
00:45:51,662 --> 00:45:55,840
These represent the most sophisticated and autonomous systems currently available.

324
00:45:56,014 --> 00:46:07,440
These agents can autonomously break down complex tasks into smaller subtasks and carry them out using the tools that are available to them, but they do so dynamically instead of just following a set of predetermined sequences.

325
00:46:07,726 --> 00:46:16,160
So, unlike workflows that follow fixed steps, agents are goal-oriented systems that can adapt their approach based on specific requirements of each situation.

326
00:46:16,400 --> 00:46:25,040
Agents are ideal for situations where it's difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps and the exact path that cannot be hard-coded in advance.

327
00:46:25,230 --> 00:46:33,520
They excel in complex decision-making environments, where they need to evaluate potential actions and choose the best approach based on current circumstances.

328
00:46:33,680 --> 00:46:42,916
This makes them particularly valuable for applications like financial trading, where market conditions constantly change and also require real-time analysis and decision-making.

329
00:46:43,310 --> 00:46:50,080
Understanding when to use each of these technologies is crucial for implementing AI solutions effectively.

330
00:46:50,320 --> 00:47:03,440
The choice between large language models, assistants, workflows, and agents depends largely on the complexity of your use case and also on how much autonomy that you need the system to actually have.

331
00:47:03,758 --> 00:47:12,388
Large language models work best for straightforward single interaction tasks where you need high-quality text generation or analysis.

332
00:47:12,560 --> 00:47:28,720
If you need to summarize documents, generate creative content, translate languages, or maybe do something like get coding assistance for specific problems, something like a basic language model is often the most efficient and cost-effective solution.

333
00:47:28,910 --> 00:47:37,200
These are perfect for initial blocks for applications involving reasoning, where you have a clear input and expect a specific type of output.

334
00:47:37,440 --> 00:47:48,080
Generative AI assistance become the better choice when you need ongoing and also interactive support that can leverage external information sources.

335
00:47:48,240 --> 00:47:53,600
Now, I want you to think about a scenario of customer support as an example.

336
00:47:53,838 --> 00:48:08,080
Something like this would be ideal for assistance because they can maintain conversation context, access company-specific information, and also they can provide personalized responses based on customer history and current policy.

337
00:48:08,302 --> 00:48:19,600
Service automation tasks can also benefit from assistance because they can integrate with existing business systems and databases to provide comprehensive support that goes beyond generic responses.

338
00:48:19,822 --> 00:48:26,640
Generative AI workflows are ideal when you have processes that can be clearly defined and easily decomposed into fixed subtasks.

339
00:48:26,800 --> 00:48:37,120
Marketing copy generation is a perfect example, because the creative process often follows predictable steps, such as research, ideation, drafting, refinement, and finalization.

340
00:48:37,326 --> 00:48:47,812
Workflows also excel in routing and parallelization scenarios, where you can increase efficiency by handling independent subtasks simultaneously or in a predetermined sequence.

341
00:48:48,078 --> 00:48:57,764
Specialized processes like customer service inquiries, where you can map out the typical resolution path, benefit greatly from the structured approach that workflows typically provide.

342
00:48:59,150 --> 00:49:07,712
Now, generative AI agents are the right choice for situations requiring the highest level of autonomy and adaptability.

343
00:49:07,872 --> 00:49:16,912
When it's difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps needed to accomplish a goal, agents can dynamically determine the best approach.

344
00:49:17,134 --> 00:49:28,292
They excel in complex decision-making environments where the system needs to evaluate multiple potential actions and choose the optimal path based on current conditions and also the available information.

345
00:49:28,846 --> 00:49:38,832
Financial trading applications exemplify this perfectly because market conditions change rapidly and also require real-time analysis, strategic thinking, and also immediate action.

346
00:49:39,072 --> 00:49:50,180
For many organizations, beginning their AI journey, starting with a prescriptive generative AI workflow, provides a more manageable and often more effective entry point than immediately deploying fully autonomous agents.

347
00:49:50,352 --> 00:49:56,324
Workflows allow you to maintain more control over the process while still gaining significant automation benefits.

348
00:49:56,512 --> 00:50:07,552
You can observe how AI handles structured tasks, refine the process based on your results, and gradually increase autonomy as you become more comfortable with the technology's capabilities as well as its limitations.

349
00:50:07,758 --> 00:50:15,232
This approach also makes it easier to integrate AI solutions with existing business processes, and also train staff on new procedures.

350
00:50:15,438 --> 00:50:24,068
Now let's take a deeper look at generative AI workflows, which occupy a crucial middle ground in our progression from basic language models to fully autonomous agents.

351
00:50:24,814 --> 00:50:33,380
Now, understanding workflows is essential because they represent one of the most practical and widely implemented applications of generative AI in business environments.

352
00:50:34,830 --> 00:50:45,572
Generative AI workflows are fundamentally different from the single interaction approach of basic language models or the free-form conversations of AI assistants.

353
00:50:45,792 --> 00:50:58,352
Instead, workflows organize complex tasks into a fixed sequence of steps, where each step is executed systematically using the language model's calls and also potentially additional tools.

354
00:50:58,606 --> 00:51:02,832
The key characteristic that defines workflows is their prescriptive nature.

355
00:51:03,086 --> 00:51:12,352
Unlike agents that can dynamically adapt their approach, workflows follow a predetermined sequence that has been carefully designed to handle specific types of tasks effectively.

356
00:51:12,512 --> 00:51:21,632
Each step in the workflow is executed by making calls to large language models, and these calls can be enhanced with additional tools and data sources as needed.

357
00:51:21,792 --> 00:51:34,432
This structured approach provides predictability and consistency, which makes workflows particularly valuable for business processes that need to maintain quality standards and compliance requirements.

358
00:51:34,702 --> 00:51:41,444
What makes workflows powerful is their ability to break down complex challenges into manageable components.

359
00:51:42,350 --> 00:51:55,552
Instead of asking a language model to handle an entire complex task in one interaction, workflows divide the work into smaller, focused steps that can be each optimized for specific purposes.

360
00:51:55,790 --> 00:52:09,316
This decomposition often leads to higher quality results because each step can be designed to excel at its particular function, and the output from one step provides focused input for the next step in the sequence.

361
00:52:09,552 --> 00:52:21,552
The systematic execution approach also allows for better error handling and quality control, since each step produces specific outputs that can be evaluated before moving into the next step.

362
00:52:21,712 --> 00:52:28,672
Workflows can include checkpoints and validation processes that ensure the overall process stays on track.

363
00:52:28,846 --> 00:52:37,792
If something goes wrong at any point, the workflow can be designed to handle errors gracefully or alert human supervisors to intervene when necessary.

364
00:52:38,062 --> 00:52:46,512
Another important aspect of workflows is their ability to incorporate both language model capabilities and external tools seamlessly.

365
00:52:46,672 --> 00:52:58,032
A single workflow might include steps that involve generating creative content, accessing databases, performing calculations, sending communications, and updating records.

366
00:52:58,192 --> 00:53:08,196
This integration capability makes workflows particularly suitable for business processes that span multiple systems and also may require coordination between different types of tasks.

367
00:53:09,326 --> 00:53:19,136
Understanding when to implement generative AI workflows requires recognizing the specific scenarios where their structured approach provides the greatest value.

368
00:53:19,376 --> 00:53:36,416
Workflows excel in situations where tasks can be easily and clearly decomposed into fixed subtasks, making them ideal for processes that follow predictable patterns while still benefiting from the intelligence and adaptability of generative AI.

369
00:53:36,656 --> 00:53:45,376
One of the primary use cases for workflows is generating marketing copy and other creative content that follows established processes.

370
00:53:45,550 --> 00:53:53,444
Marketing content creation typically involves research phases, where you gather information about target audiences and also the competitive landscape;

371
00:53:53,678 --> 00:53:57,936
the ideation phases, where you brainstorm concepts and messaging approaches;

372
00:53:58,256 --> 00:54:00,976
drafting phases, where you create initial content;

373
00:54:01,296 --> 00:54:05,776
refinement phases, where you optimize for brand voice and messaging effectiveness;

374
00:54:05,936 --> 00:54:10,336
and finalization phases, where you format and prepare content for distribution.

375
00:54:10,510 --> 00:54:19,524
Each of these phases can be handled by specialized workflow steps that build upon the previous work while also maintaining consistency and quality throughout the process.

376
00:54:21,166 --> 00:54:32,416
Workflows are also excellent for routing and parallelization scenarios, where you can increase overall effectiveness by handling independent subtasks simultaneously or in optimized sequences.

377
00:54:32,576 --> 00:54:49,456
As an example, a customer service workflow might simultaneously research customer history, they might also do something like check inventory status, and also review relevant policies before synthesizing this information into a comprehensive response.

378
00:54:49,696 --> 00:54:59,296
This parallel processing approach can significantly reduce response times while also ensuring that all relevant factors are considered in the final solution.

379
00:54:59,662 --> 00:55:07,776
Specialized processes, particularly those designed for customer service inquiries, benefit greatly from the structured approach that workflows provide.

380
00:55:08,016 --> 00:55:22,336
Customer service interactions often follow predictable patterns where you need to verify customer identity, understand the specific issue, research relevant information, apply company policies, and also provide appropriate solutions.

381
00:55:22,606 --> 00:55:37,796
While each customer interaction is unique, the underlying process remains consistent enough that a well-designed workflow can handle the majority of cases effectively, while escalating unusual situations to something like human agents.

382
00:55:38,096 --> 00:55:52,292
The foundational nature of workflows makes them particularly valuable for building multi-step generative AI applications that need to maintain consistency as well as, to a large extent, reliability.

383
00:55:53,678 --> 00:56:04,096
Unlike agents that might approach the same problem differently each time, workflows ensure that similar inputs produce similar processes as well as outcomes.

384
00:56:04,366 --> 00:56:15,364
This predictability is crucial for business applications where consistency in customer experience, compliance and regulations, and maintainability of the system are important considerations.

385
00:56:15,886 --> 00:56:33,924
Now, an essential insight for organizations considering AI implementation is that generative AI workflows are prescriptive and also represent a predefined sequence of steps that can be automated using generative AI technologies combined with language models and optional tool calls.

386
00:56:34,096 --> 00:56:44,096
This prescriptive nature means that workflows require more upfront planning and design compared to agents, but they also provide more control and predictability in their execution.

387
00:56:44,334 --> 00:56:55,536
For many business applications, this trade off between flexibility and control makes workflows the optimal choice for achieving reliable automation while also maintaining the benefits of advanced AI capabilities.

388
00:56:55,856 --> 00:57:00,868
You now know what an AI agent actually is and why it matters right now.

389
00:57:01,262 --> 00:57:02,916
Next, let's open one up.

390
00:57:03,342 --> 00:57:06,336
We'll look at the actual pieces that make an agent work.

391
00:57:06,768 --> 00:57:17,088
And let's examine one of the most fundamental and widely used design patterns for building generative AI workflows, the sequence pattern, also known as prompt chaining.

392
00:57:17,248 --> 00:57:25,568
This pattern represents a systematic approach to constructing sophisticated AI workflows that can handle complex tasks through a series of carefully orchestrated steps.

393
00:57:25,742 --> 00:57:35,568
Think of the sequential pattern as an assembly line for language models, where each stage has a specific purpose, and the output of one stage directly feeds into the neck.

394
00:57:35,728 --> 00:57:45,008
This approach allows you to break down complex tasks into a series of more manageable and focused operations, each optimized for a particular aspect of the overall objective.

395
00:57:45,262 --> 00:57:59,204
Just like a manufacturing assembly line where each worker specializes in one specific task, each step in a sequential workflow focuses on one particular aspect of the problem, leading to higher quality and also more reliable results.

396
00:57:59,408 --> 00:58:09,968
The architecture of this pattern follows a linear flow that begins with an input, which could be a user query or a piece of data, or an initial prompt that sets the entire workflow in motion.

397
00:58:10,222 --> 00:58:15,968
This input is then processed by the first language model, which handles the initial stage of the task.

398
00:58:16,238 --> 00:58:29,888
What makes this pattern particularly powerful is that any step in the sequence can also involve external tools to retrieve information or perform specific actions, with the results feeding into the next language model, called in the chain.

399
00:58:30,254 --> 00:58:38,468
A crucial element that distinguishes the sequential pattern from simpler approaches is the incorporation of built-in check mechanisms between the steps.

400
00:58:39,150 --> 00:58:46,368
These checks act as a quality control point that evaluates the output from each preceding step against a predefined criteria.

401
00:58:46,542 --> 00:59:00,848
These validation steps might involve checking for factual accuracy, ensuring adherence to specific formatting guidelines, performing sentiment analysis to verify appropriate tone, or applying other relevant validation based on the specific requirements of your task.

402
00:59:01,008 --> 00:59:04,768
The outcome of each check determines the subsequent path of the workflow.

403
00:59:04,928 --> 00:59:12,368
If the output passes the validation criteria, it proceeds to the next stage, where a language model performs further processing.

404
00:59:12,528 --> 00:59:21,168
This demonstrates the true chaining aspect of the pattern, where refined and also validate output from one step serves as input for the subsequent stage.

405
00:59:21,358 --> 00:59:33,156
However, if the output fails the check, the workflow can exit gracefully, triggering error, handling mechanisms or prompting for the revised input, instead of just blindly proceeding, with potentially flawed results.

406
00:59:33,422 --> 00:59:36,768
This structured approach offers several significant advantages.

407
00:59:36,974 --> 00:59:42,048
Accuracy is enhanced by assigning simpler, well-defined subtasks to each language model, call.

408
00:59:42,478 --> 00:59:54,288
Instead of just asking a single model to handle the entire complexity all at once, this divide and conquer principle leads to more reliable results because each step can be optimized for a specific function.

409
00:59:54,510 --> 01:00:04,608
The built-in checks prevent deviation from the main objective, and also provide early detection of issues, stop in problems before they compound through the subsequent steps.

410
01:00:04,846 --> 01:00:11,328
The sequential pattern is best suited for tasks that can clearly be decomposed into a fixed sequence of substeps.

411
01:00:11,568 --> 01:00:15,408
Consider the example of generating a comprehensive blog post for a marketing team.

412
01:00:15,534 --> 01:00:24,608
The workflow might begin with the initial language model call focused on generating a first draft that covers all of the necessary points and key information.

413
01:00:24,814 --> 01:00:33,328
This draft then passes through a content accuracy check that validates factual information and ensures completeness against the predefined requirements.

414
01:00:33,550 --> 01:00:41,408
If the draft passes this validation, a second language model call focuses specifically on the refinement and style improvement.

415
01:00:41,568 --> 01:00:48,208
It also works on enhancing readability, improving flow, and ensuring the tone aligns with the brand guidelines.

416
01:00:48,462 --> 01:00:59,728
This refined version might then undergo another check for grammar as well as consistency before proceeding to a final language model call that optimizes the content for search engines, and also adds compelling calls to action.

417
01:00:59,950 --> 01:01:09,408
Each step in the sequence has a clear, focused purpose, and the validation points ensure that quality issues are caught and addressed before they can affect subsequent steps.

418
01:01:09,678 --> 01:01:17,408
If any check fails, the workflow can exit gracefully, potentially notifying human editors, or providing specific feedback for improvement.

419
01:01:17,568 --> 01:01:32,128
This systematic approach transforms a complex creative task into manageable, repeatable processes that consistently produce high quality results while also maintaining the creative capabilities that make generative AI valuable for content creation.

420
01:01:51,118 --> 01:01:59,152
Moving on, we will now delve into another crucial design pattern for constructing effective, generative AI workflows, the parallelization pattern.

421
01:01:59,392 --> 01:02:09,152
As the name suggests, this pattern is centered around the idea of executing multiple tasks or processes concurrently to achieve faster results or gather a broader range of insights.

422
01:02:09,312 --> 01:02:12,912
Let's first examine the visual representation on the slide.

423
01:02:13,152 --> 01:02:18,112
You can see here that there is an initial input that serves as the starting point for the workflow.

424
01:02:18,318 --> 01:02:22,272
This input is then directed into three distinct and parallel pathways.

425
01:02:22,446 --> 01:02:27,872
Each contains an LLM block labeled as call 1, call 2, and call 3.

426
01:02:28,032 --> 01:02:39,392
This illustrates that the initial input is processed simultaneously by multiple instances of large language models or potentially by different tools based on the specific requirements of each parallel task.

427
01:02:39,598 --> 01:02:57,444
The OR tool output notation is important as it signifies that each parallel LML call can also independently leverage various tools to gather information, perform specific operations, or interact with external systems, as we discussed in the context of AI agents having access to tools.

428
01:02:57,678 --> 01:03:04,912
Following these parallel LM calls, the output from each of these independent branches converge into a central aggregator block.

429
01:03:05,166 --> 01:03:13,792
The role of this aggregator is to take the individual outputs generated by each parallel process and combine them in a meaningful way to produce a final output.

430
01:03:13,966 --> 01:03:18,592
The specific function of the aggregator will depend heavily on the nature of the parallel task.

431
01:03:18,798 --> 01:03:29,872
It might involve summarizing the findings from each branch, selecting the most relevant information, consolidating different perspectives, or performing a final analysis based on the combined results.

432
01:03:30,158 --> 01:03:36,484
The core approach of the parallelization pattern lies in the simultaneous execution of independent subtasks.

433
01:03:37,102 --> 01:03:49,472
Instead of processing tasks sequentially, this pattern allows for division of larger objectives into smaller, self-contained units, it allows them to be easily worked on at the same time.

434
01:03:49,710 --> 01:03:57,392
This concurrency can significantly reduce the overall processing time, especially when dealing with tasks that are not dependent on one another.

435
01:03:57,582 --> 01:04:12,032
Furthermore, by leveraging multiple LLM calls or tools in parallel, this pattern can also facilitate the gathering of diverse information or the application of different analytical perspectives of the same input.

436
01:04:12,238 --> 01:04:20,192
The subsequent aggregation step ensures that these independent efforts are synthesized into a unified and coherent final result.

437
01:04:20,462 --> 01:04:25,792
Let's consider a practical business scenario where the parallelization pattern would highly be effective.

438
01:04:26,032 --> 01:04:38,468
Imagine a company wanting to comprehensively assess customer sentiment towards a newly launched product based on a large volume of social media posts, online reviews, and also customer support tickets.

439
01:04:38,766 --> 01:04:51,312
Instead of processing all of this data sequentially, we could employ a parallelization strategy, and what we could do is we could have three parallel LLMs, each tasked with analyzing a different aspect of customer sentiment.

440
01:04:51,472 --> 01:05:10,032
For example, we could have LLM call one, which is sentiment analysis, and this branch could be responsible for performing a general sentiment analysis on the entire data set, categorizing each piece of feedback as positive, negative, or neutral, and potentially identifying main topics associated with each sentiment.

441
01:05:10,192 --> 01:05:14,032
It might also use a dedicated sentiment analysis tool for this purpose.

442
01:05:14,350 --> 01:05:27,152
LLM call 2, which could be the aspect-based sentiment analysis, and the idea behind this parallel process is that it could focus on identifying specific aspects or features of the product that's mentioned in the feedback.

443
01:05:27,312 --> 01:05:35,620
For example, battery life, user interface, performance, etc., and then determining the sentiment expressed towards each of these individual aspects.

444
01:05:35,854 --> 01:05:44,832
This could involve a more granular analysis and also might utilize tools that are specialized in aspect extraction and also sentiment scoring.

445
01:05:45,070 --> 01:05:49,872
Now, LLM Call 3, this could be something like trend identification, and this branch could.

446
01:05:50,336 --> 01:05:54,736
Analyze the feedback over time to identify emerging trends in customer opinion.

447
01:05:54,896 --> 01:06:01,616
Maybe it might pinpoint any significant shifts in sentiment or the rising prominence of particular issues or praises.

448
01:06:01,998 --> 01:06:05,376
It might use data analysis tool to visualize these trends over time.

449
01:06:05,550 --> 01:06:11,776
Now, once these parallel analyses are complete, the aggregator would then combine the outputs from each of these branches.

450
01:06:11,982 --> 01:06:27,216
Now, this could involve generating a comprehensive report that not only provides an overall sentiment score, but also highlights the specific aspects that are driving positive, negative, and also identifying any significant trends in customer opinion over time.

451
01:06:27,438 --> 01:06:38,256
This parallel approach allows the company to gain a much richer and also much more nuanced understanding of customer sentiment in a significantly shorter time frame compared to a sequential analysis.

452
01:06:38,446 --> 01:06:50,976
The parallelization pattern is ideally suited for scenarios where the rapid processing is essential or when the objective requires gathering varied insights or performing multiple independent analysis on the same input.

453
01:06:51,182 --> 01:06:59,536
Now, the example provided here in the slide is concurrently applying LLM as a judge to assess performance against several distinct metrics at the same time.

454
01:06:59,776 --> 01:07:02,336
Now, it perfectly illustrates this idea.

455
01:07:02,576 --> 01:07:11,696
Imagine evaluating the quality of a generated piece of content against criteria like factual accuracy, grammatical correctness, clarity, and style.

456
01:07:11,936 --> 01:07:22,656
By having separate LLM calls act as judges for each criterion in parallel, we can obtain a multifaceted evaluation much faster than if we were to assess each criterion sequentially.

457
01:07:22,862 --> 01:07:30,788
The aggregator would then combine these individual evaluations to provide an overall quality, score, and specific feedback on each aspect.

458
01:07:31,022 --> 01:07:46,468
So in summary, the parallelization pattern offers a powerful way to enhance the efficiency and also the comprehensiveness of generative AI workflows by enabling the simultaneous execution of independent tasks and also the subsequent aggregation of their results.

459
01:07:46,702 --> 01:07:59,296
This pattern is particularly valuable when dealing with complex problems that can be broken down into parallelable subproblems, or when a holistic understanding requires integration of diverse perspectives or analysis.

460
01:07:59,598 --> 01:08:07,296
Continuing our exploration of Gen AI workflow design patterns, let's now delve into the conditional pattern, also known as routing.

461
01:08:07,456 --> 01:08:17,696
This pattern introduces a crucial element of decision-making into our workflows, allowing them to adapt and also respond differently based on the specific conditions or the nature of the input.

462
01:08:17,936 --> 01:08:22,896
At the heart of a conditional pattern lies a decision point that acts as a router.

463
01:08:23,216 --> 01:08:30,816
Instead of following a fixed sequence of steps, the workflow evaluates a condition or examines the input at this juncture.

464
01:08:31,086 --> 01:08:39,776
Based on the outcome of this evaluation, the workflow is then directed down one of several potential paths, each designed to handle a specific scenario.

465
01:08:40,016 --> 01:08:42,656
This is visually represented here on the slide.

466
01:08:42,830 --> 01:08:47,216
As we can see over here, we have call 1 or tool output that's feeding into a control.

467
01:08:47,456 --> 01:08:48,416
Decision box.

468
01:08:48,654 --> 01:08:54,736
From this control decision box, arrows branch out, indicating different possible routes that the workflow can take.

469
01:08:54,896 --> 01:09:00,336
In the depicted example, there are two such branches, each leading to a subsequent LLM call.

470
01:09:00,590 --> 01:09:03,616
Call 2 or tool output, and call 3 or tool output.

471
01:09:03,776 --> 01:09:10,256
Finally, a conditional aggregator is shown receiving input from these different branches and producing the final output.

472
01:09:10,478 --> 01:09:22,576
The dashed line leading into the LLM, or the LLM with call 3, emphasizes that only one of these paths will be active based on the decision that's going to be made.

473
01:09:22,736 --> 01:09:33,136
The fundamental approach of the conditional pattern is to introduce a structured method for handling variations in the tasks or input within a gen AI workflow.

474
01:09:33,296 --> 01:09:40,976
This is particularly useful when dealing with diverse types of information or different processing steps are required based on the specific criteria.

475
01:09:41,262 --> 01:09:50,816
The initial LLM call in this pattern might be responsible for understanding the input and extracting relevant information that's needed for the subsequent writing decision.

476
01:09:51,022 --> 01:09:59,296
The control decision component then takes this information and applies a set of rules or logic to determine which path the workflow should follow.

477
01:09:59,456 --> 01:10:04,896
This decision could be based on keywords identified in the input, the output of a previous step.

478
01:10:05,376 --> 01:10:07,936
Or data retrieved from an external source.

479
01:10:08,206 --> 01:10:14,816
Each of these subsequent branches represents a specialized sequence of operations that are tailored to handle a particular condition.

480
01:10:14,990 --> 01:10:26,336
The conditional aggregator plays the role of collecting the output from whichever branch was executed and also potentially formatting into a unified final response.

481
01:10:26,606 --> 01:10:33,536
The conditional pattern is ideally suited for scenarios where you need to handle distinct categories of tasks or input separately.

482
01:10:33,776 --> 01:10:37,572
A prime example of this is managing different types of customer queries.

483
01:10:37,776 --> 01:10:44,736
Imagine a customer service chatbot powered by a gen AI workflow employing the conditional pattern.

484
01:10:44,974 --> 01:10:54,496
When a customer submits a query, the initial step that's represented by LLM Call 1 could involve analyzing the customer's message to understand their intent.

485
01:10:54,656 --> 01:10:59,936
The control decision component would then use this understanding to categorize the query.

486
01:11:00,176 --> 01:11:12,016
For instance, if the query contains keywords like refund, return, or cancel order, control decision might route the workflow down a path specifically designed to handle refund and return.

487
01:11:12,176 --> 01:11:22,496
This path, LLM call 2, could involve assessing order information, checking the company refund policy, and also potentially initiating the refund process.

488
01:11:22,670 --> 01:11:39,776
On the other hand, if the initial analysis identifies key words related to technical issues, such as not working, error, or installation, the control decision would then direct the workflow to a different path, LLM Call 3, and this is focused on technical support.

489
01:11:39,936 --> 01:11:49,296
This technical support path might involve assessing knowledge bases, troubleshooting, guides, or even connecting the customer with a human support agent.

490
01:11:49,614 --> 01:11:56,176
Similar queries related to order tracking or product information could be routed to their respective specialized branches.

491
01:11:56,366 --> 01:12:08,736
The conditional aggregator would then ensure that the response, whether it's confirmation of a refund, troubleshooting steps, or order tracking details, is presented to the customer in a clear and consistent manner.

492
01:12:08,896 --> 01:12:22,096
This ability to route different types of inquiries to tailored processing paths significantly improves the efficiency and effectiveness of Gen AI workflows, leading to more accurate and relevant responses for the users.

493
01:12:22,286 --> 01:12:33,296
In summary, the conditional pattern routing empowers Gen AI workflows with the ability to make decisions and adapt their executions based on specific conditions or input.

494
01:12:33,456 --> 01:12:50,816
By employing a decision point to direct the flow to specialized branches, this pattern enables the efficient and accurate handling of diverse tasks and scenarios, as illustrated, by the customer service chatbot example, where different query types are routed to dedicate processing paths.

495
01:12:50,990 --> 01:12:58,404
This design pattern enhances the flexibility and intelligence of Gen AI workflows, making them more capable of addressing real-world complexities.

496
01:13:18,702 --> 01:13:25,240
Now, let's examine how actions are orchestrated in AI agent systems through the strategic use of tools.

497
01:13:26,380 --> 01:13:35,600
Now, this is where the theoretical planning that we discussed earlier transforms into concrete and measurable results that can interact with real-world systems and data.

498
01:13:41,646 --> 01:13:51,076
The fundamental concept here is that actions within AI agents are orchestrated by allowing these systems to access and as well as utilize specialized tools.

499
01:13:52,110 --> 01:13:57,220
Think of this like giving a skilled craftsperson access to a fully equipped workshop.

500
01:13:58,190 --> 01:14:03,428
Now, this craftsperson has the knowledge as well as expertise to create something valuable.

501
01:14:04,016 --> 01:14:08,420
But without the right tools, that knowledge remains purely theoretical.

502
01:14:09,454 --> 01:14:23,588
Similarly, a large language model has impressive reasoning capabilities, but it needs tools to translate those capabilities into meaningful actions that can affect change in the real world.

503
01:14:25,536 --> 01:14:31,524
Let me walk you through a practical example that demonstrates this orchestration in action.

504
01:14:31,918 --> 01:14:36,068
Now I want you to imagine that a customer contacts a company with a specific query.

505
01:14:36,526 --> 01:14:40,388
Maybe something like, hi, my order ORD123, has it been shipped?

506
01:14:41,006 --> 01:14:42,372
If not, can I get a refund?

507
01:14:43,022 --> 01:14:49,316
Now this seems like a simple question, but it actually requires a sophisticated sequence of actions to actually resolve properly.

508
01:14:50,222 --> 01:14:55,748
Now, when this input reaches the agent, the planning and reasoning phase immediately begins.

509
01:14:56,366 --> 01:14:59,364
So it starts by analyzing what needs to be accomplished.

510
01:15:00,302 --> 01:15:14,468
The agent recognizes that this isn't just a one simple task kind of thing, but instead it's a multi-step process that requires gathering information, making decisions based on that information, and then taking appropriate action.

511
01:15:15,278 --> 01:15:17,444
So the agent formulates a clear strategy.

512
01:15:18,030 --> 01:15:19,856
It's to address this query.

513
01:15:20,046 --> 01:15:22,660
I need to access the following tools in sequence.

514
01:15:23,406 --> 01:15:29,380
First is to get order details, then tracking shipping, then initiate the refund.

515
01:15:29,934 --> 01:15:33,380
Refund should be initiated only if the order is not shipped.

516
01:15:34,158 --> 01:15:37,540
So notice how the agent doesn't just randomly start using the tools.

517
01:15:37,966 --> 01:15:43,364
Instead, it creates a logical sequence that ensures that each step builds upon the previous one.

518
01:15:44,142 --> 01:15:51,524
Now this is crucial because the information gathered from early steps directly influences what actions should be taken in later steps.

519
01:15:51,776 --> 01:15:55,204
The action phase then begins executing this plan systematically.

520
01:15:55,758 --> 01:16:02,980
The agent starts by accessing the Get Order Details tool, providing it with the order number ORD 123.

521
01:16:03,822 --> 01:16:13,668
Now, this tool, which is implemented as a function call behind the scenes, requires the company's database and also returns specific information about that order.

522
01:16:14,798 --> 01:16:19,972
The agent then uses the track shipping tool to determine the current status of the shipment.

523
01:16:20,846 --> 01:16:29,376
Now, in this particular example, the tracking system indicates that shipping information is available and it also provides a tracking number.

524
01:16:29,614 --> 01:16:32,612
And so in this case it's TRK456.

525
01:16:34,126 --> 01:16:38,116
Now, here's where the agent's reasoning capabilities truly shine.

526
01:16:39,086 --> 01:16:45,988
So based on the information gathered from these tools, the agent recognizes that the order has indeed been already shipped.

527
01:16:46,734 --> 01:16:54,500
Now this means that the customer's request for a refund, which was conditional on the order not being shipped, should not be processed.

528
01:16:55,278 --> 01:17:02,052
Instead, the agent can provide the customer with the tracking information that they need to monitor their shipment.

529
01:17:02,446 --> 01:17:08,356
The tool inventory shown in this example here represents the complete set of capabilities available to the agent.

530
01:17:08,974 --> 01:17:18,916
So these tools include things like get order details, check inventory, track shipping, initiate refund, request customer feedback, and also escalate issues.

531
01:17:20,782 --> 01:17:28,772
So each of these tools serves a specific business function and they can also be combined in different ways to handle various other customer scenarios.

532
01:17:29,998 --> 01:17:37,636
The power of this approach lies in the agent's ability to select the right combination of tools for each unique situation.

533
01:17:38,926 --> 01:17:51,364
Now, it is important to understand that these tools are implemented as function calls, with each function containing internal logic that represents specific business tasks.

534
01:17:52,046 --> 01:18:02,500
For example, the GetOrderDetails tool might actually be implemented as a Python function, and this essentially queries a database for order information.

535
01:18:03,694 --> 01:18:12,004
The track shipping tool might connect to a shipping company's API to retrieve real-time tracking data.

536
01:18:13,006 --> 01:18:22,084
So each tool encapsulates complex business logic behind a simple interface that the agent can easily understand as well as utilize.

537
01:18:23,598 --> 01:18:32,356
Now the final output demonstrates the agent's ability to synthesize information from multiple tools into a coherent and helpful response.

538
01:18:33,294 --> 01:18:38,532
In this case, the response is your order, ORD123, has already shipped.

539
01:18:39,086 --> 01:18:42,436
You can track it using the tracking number TRK456.

540
01:18:43,278 --> 01:18:47,844
If you have any further questions or need assistance, feel free to ask.

541
01:18:48,462 --> 01:19:00,996
So this response shows that the agent not only executed the necessary tools, but it also applied business logic to determine the appropriate course of action based on the information that it ended up gathering.

542
01:19:02,158 --> 01:19:10,948
So this orchestration approach enables AI agents to handle complex and multi-step processes that require both reasoning as well as action.

543
01:19:11,824 --> 01:19:24,644
The agent serves as an intelligent coordinator that understands business processes, selects appropriate tools, sequences those tools logically, and then it synthesizes the results into meaningful outcomes.

544
01:19:26,094 --> 01:19:36,784
Now this transforms static AI models into dynamic systems that are capable of handling real-world business scenarios with sophistication as well as reliability.

545
01:19:36,974 --> 01:19:42,660
Now we need to address a critical challenge that emerges when building sophisticated AI agent systems.

546
01:19:43,534 --> 01:19:46,820
The problem of interoperable agent tooling.

547
01:19:47,854 --> 01:20:00,196
Now, this challenge becomes apparent when we examine the plan action interface, which is the crucial connection point between an agent's thinking process and its ability to execute real-world tasks.

548
01:20:01,518 --> 01:20:10,820
Now, let me explain what we mean by the plan action interface and also why this presents as such a significant challenge.

549
01:20:12,110 --> 01:20:29,188
The plan action interface represents the communication pathway between an agent's planning module where it decides what needs to be done, and its action module, where it actually executes those decisions using the set of available tools.

550
01:20:30,286 --> 01:20:39,972
Now you can kind of think of this like the connection between your brain deciding to write a letter and your hand actually holding the pen and forming the words.

551
01:20:41,326 --> 01:20:49,764
The interface between the intention and execution must be seamless as well as reliable for the system to work efficiently.

552
01:20:51,342 --> 01:21:01,188
Now, the challenge of interoperable agent tooling becomes clear when we examine how different AI frameworks implement this interface.

553
01:21:02,094 --> 01:21:07,620
Now consider two popular agent frameworks Langgraph and Crew AI.

554
01:21:09,102 --> 01:21:25,284
Both of these frameworks are designed to create intelligent agents that can plan and also execute actions, and both might need to access the same underlying system, such as Google Search for Information Retrieval or a Postgres database for data storage.

555
01:21:26,286 --> 01:21:39,588
However, the way that each framework handles the plan action interface is implementation specific, meaning that they use different approaches, protocols, as well as data formats.

556
01:21:41,038 --> 01:21:48,100
Now, this creates a significant problem that is illustrated by the red X between the two implementations.

557
01:21:49,422 --> 01:22:06,404
Even though both the Line Graph implementation and the Crew AI implementation need to perform similar tasks and also access the same underlying systems, the problem here is that they cannot easily share tools or communicate with each other.

558
01:22:07,438 --> 01:22:15,268
So it's kind of like having two brilliant specialists who speak different languages trying to collaborate on a project together.

559
01:22:16,174 --> 01:22:29,412
Now, they might have access to the same resources and they also want to accomplish similar goals, but their inability to communicate effectively creates barriers to collaboration as well as resource sharing.

560
01:22:29,776 --> 01:22:40,228
Now, the planning and action modules are absolutely essential to an agent's functionality because they involve frequent back and forth interactions.

561
01:22:41,838 --> 01:22:51,428
Now, during a typical problem solving session, an agent might cycle between planning and action dozens or even hundreds of times.

562
01:22:51,758 --> 01:22:56,420
The planning modules analyze the current situation and decides what action to take next.

563
01:22:57,230 --> 01:23:01,188
The action module executes that decision and reports back the results.

564
01:23:02,030 --> 01:23:07,460
The planning module then incorporates those results into its understanding and decides on the next action.

565
01:23:08,206 --> 01:23:20,900
So this continuous dialogue between planning and action is really what enables agents to adapt to their approach based on real-world feedback and it can also handle complex multi-step problems effectively.

566
01:23:22,254 --> 01:23:30,176
However, tool execution in the action module and its integration with planning are often implementation specific.

567
01:23:30,350 --> 01:23:43,268
Now this means that different agent frameworks have developed their own unique ways of defining tools, calling tools, passing parameters to tools, and also interpreting tool results.

568
01:23:44,558 --> 01:23:55,844
So these implementation details, they might seem like minor technical considerations, but they do end up creating significant barriers to interoperability.

569
01:23:56,430 --> 01:24:05,124
So when each framework uses its own conventions and protocols, tools developed for one framework cannot easily be used by another framework.

570
01:24:06,222 --> 01:24:11,204
Now, this does end up leading us to a particularly frustrating situation.

571
01:24:12,270 --> 01:24:26,788
Even though the same tools might be conceptually useful across different agent frameworks, the implementation-specific details prevent the plan action interface from one framework being used with another framework.

572
01:24:27,502 --> 01:24:40,708
So for example, a Google search tool that's developed for line graph cannot simply be plugged into a crew AI agent, even though both agents might benefit from the ability to search the same internet.

573
01:24:41,966 --> 01:24:54,308
Similarly, a database connection tool created for one framework requires significant modification or maybe even a complete re implementation to work with a different framework.

574
01:24:56,206 --> 01:25:04,900
So, this lack of interoperability creates several problems for developers and also organizations building AI agent systems.

575
01:25:06,158 --> 01:25:14,180
First, it leads to duplicated effort as the same tools must be re-implemented multiple times for different frameworks.

576
01:25:15,630 --> 01:25:28,644
Second, it creates vendor lock-in, where choosing one agent framework means that you're now completely committed to using only tools that are capable or compatible with that framework.

577
01:25:29,390 --> 01:25:39,712
Third, it slows down innovation as developers are now trying to spend more time on technical integration instead of just focusing on solving business problems.

578
01:25:39,982 --> 01:25:49,316
Finally, it fragments the AI agent ecosystem, preventing the kind of collaborative tool development that could really benefit the entire community.

579
01:25:50,510 --> 01:26:08,068
So the need for interoperable agent tooling has become increasingly urgent as AI agents become more sophisticated and also as organizations want to integrate multiple agent frameworks or switch between frameworks without losing their existing tool investments.

580
01:26:09,422 --> 01:26:15,712
Now, this challenge has motivated the development of standardized approaches to agent tooling.

