918
02:01:51,694 --> 02:01:57,828
We use request post function to send the POST request with the URL, headers, and payload.

919
02:01:58,382 --> 02:02:02,448
If the response is successful, it gives the status code 200.

920
02:02:02,734 --> 02:02:04,612
Then we can proceed with Langchain.

921
02:02:05,166 --> 02:02:13,604
The response status code shows whether the request was successful, and response JSON function prints the model's response to the test message.

922
02:02:14,318 --> 02:02:17,380
Next, we move forward with the Langchain's chain.

923
02:02:17,774 --> 02:02:23,044
In the code, after setting up authentication, we move on to using Langchain itself.

924
02:02:23,662 --> 02:02:26,404
First, we need to set the environment variables.

925
02:02:26,830 --> 02:02:32,768
We begin by storing the OpenRouter API key and base URL in environment variables.

926
02:02:33,006 --> 02:02:39,972
These environment variables will be used later to authenticate the API request and communicate with the OpenRouter service.

927
02:02:40,910 --> 02:02:43,428
Then we need to import required libraries.

928
02:02:43,982 --> 02:02:46,820
One is ChatOpenAI and Prompt Template.

929
02:02:47,630 --> 02:03:02,212
Here, Chat OpenAI enables interaction with OpenAI's models via Langchain, while Prompt Template allows us to create reusable and dynamic input prompts for the model, ensuring consistency and reducing redundancy.

930
02:03:02,638 --> 02:03:15,908
Without Prompt Template, we would need to manually define the prompt structure every time we want to interact with the model, which can be tedious and prone to errors, especially when dealing with multiple inputs or complex tasks.

931
02:03:16,622 --> 02:03:18,820
Next, we create the model instance.

932
02:03:19,310 --> 02:03:21,968
We instantiate the Chat OpenAI model.

933
02:03:22,638 --> 02:03:33,572
Here you can see the model is set to Open Router Auto, and we use the Open Router API key and base URL from the environment variables to configure the connection.

934
02:03:34,062 --> 02:03:42,340
And here, temperature equal to 0.7 controls the creativity of the model's output, which ensures the balanced responses.

935
02:03:42,798 --> 02:03:44,996
Next, we define the prompt template.

936
02:03:45,518 --> 02:03:48,868
This template instructs the model to summarize the provided text.

937
02:03:49,454 --> 02:03:54,788
Here, with text here in curly brackets being replaced by the actual input when invoked.

938
02:03:55,598 --> 02:03:59,648
And finally, the chain is created by combining the prompt and model.

939
02:04:00,302 --> 02:04:08,932
Here, the vertical bar operator in Langchain ensures that the output of the prompt is passed as input to the model, creating a workflow.

940
02:04:09,454 --> 02:04:15,332
We then invoke the chain with the input text to get the summary, and then we print the generated summary.

941
02:04:15,886 --> 02:04:19,460
So here you can see we have generated the summary of the content given.

942
02:04:19,950 --> 02:04:22,052
Now let's move forward working with agents.

943
02:04:22,318 --> 02:04:30,692
As we discussed in Langchain, an agent is an intelligent system that can decide which actions to take based on the input it receives.

944
02:04:31,726 --> 02:04:35,396
Agents can be programmed to use various tools to complete tasks.

945
02:04:36,174 --> 02:04:41,636
So in this code, first we begin by installing the necessary Python packages.

946
02:04:42,350 --> 02:04:48,580
First, Langchain, which is a core library that helps build workflows driven by language models.

947
02:04:49,294 --> 02:04:55,908
It allows us to create powerful integrations with AI models, enabling the automation of various tasks.

948
02:04:56,814 --> 02:04:59,236
Then, Langchain OpenAI.

949
02:04:59,758 --> 02:05:09,060
This module integrates OpenAI models like GPT into Langchain workflows, making it easier to use the language models within our workflow.

950
02:05:09,806 --> 02:05:12,004
Then comes Langchain Community.

951
02:05:12,494 --> 02:05:20,292
This is an extension that adds extra tools and functionalities to Langchain, which might be useful for more advanced use cases.

952
02:05:20,654 --> 02:05:24,388
After installing these packages, we import the required libraries.

953
02:05:24,910 --> 02:05:28,676
First is Chat OpenAI from Langchain OpenAI.

954
02:05:29,166 --> 02:05:33,508
This class allows interaction with OpenAI models such as GPT.

955
02:05:34,094 --> 02:05:42,916
It enables us to perform tasks like text manipulation and much more by integrating the power of OpenAI models into our workflows.

956
02:05:43,438 --> 02:05:48,676
Then we start by creating the LLM that powers the agent using Chat OpenAI.

957
02:05:49,102 --> 02:05:56,548
This is a critical step because we instantiate the model that helps the agent understand tasks and generate appropriate responses.

958
02:05:57,102 --> 02:06:09,188
In this case, we are using GPT 3.5 TurboModel, a powerful OpenAI language model known for its strong understanding and ability to generate coherent responses.

959
02:06:09,646 --> 02:06:18,436
The API key is retrieved from the environment variable, allowing the agent to securely authenticate and interact with OpenAI's API.

960
02:06:18,766 --> 02:06:23,268
Next, we define the functions that the agent will use to perform specific tasks.

961
02:06:24,238 --> 02:06:26,148
First is capitalized text.

962
02:06:26,830 --> 02:06:32,132
This function will capitalize the input text, transforming all characters into uppercase.

963
02:06:32,528 --> 02:06:35,268
Then, next function is reverse text.

964
02:06:35,854 --> 02:06:39,876
This function will reverse the input text, turning the string around.

965
02:06:40,142 --> 02:06:45,316
These functions act as tools for the agent, allowing it to perform the task when required.

966
02:06:45,998 --> 02:06:50,980
In next step, we define the agent's behavior through a function called agentLogic.

967
02:06:51,566 --> 02:06:58,596
This function processes the input text and decides which function to call, either capitalize or reverse.

968
02:06:59,566 --> 02:07:04,388
If the input asks to capitalize, it will call the capitalizeText function.

969
02:07:05,038 --> 02:07:09,284
If the input asks to reverse, it will call the reverse text function.

970
02:07:10,094 --> 02:07:12,132
Next step is testing the agent.

971
02:07:12,782 --> 02:07:16,612
We test the agent by asking it to perform two simple tasks.

972
02:07:17,262 --> 02:07:19,716
Task one is capitalize a sentence.

973
02:07:20,398 --> 02:07:26,180
Here, the agent will use the capitalized text function to convert the provided text to uppercase.

974
02:07:27,118 --> 02:07:31,652
The next one is the reverse text function to reverse the provided sentence.

975
02:07:32,622 --> 02:07:40,804
We use the agent logic function to simulate the behavior of the agent, which processes the input and returns the appropriate output.

976
02:07:41,454 --> 02:07:48,768
So in the output, we can see the agent has capitalized the given sentence and also it has reversed the given sentence.

977
02:07:49,038 --> 02:07:52,612
So the agent has done its task according to the functions given.

978
02:07:53,070 --> 02:07:55,076
So the agent is not rule-based.

979
02:07:55,598 --> 02:08:00,900
It analyzes the input and selects the most appropriate function based on the task at hand.

980
02:08:01,614 --> 02:08:05,444
This autonomy allows it to handle various tasks efficiently.

981
02:08:06,350 --> 02:08:11,396
You can easily extend the agent's capabilities by adding more functions or tasks.

982
02:08:12,206 --> 02:08:21,380
For instance, you could add additional functions for mathematical operations, other text manipulations, or even complex workflows.

983
02:08:22,190 --> 02:08:27,808
Agents allows for more autonomous behavior compared to simple prompt response system.

984
02:08:28,558 --> 02:08:32,644
Now, let's see how the output parser works with the agent's responses.

985
02:08:33,294 --> 02:08:39,716
So here first we have imported the STR output parser from Langchain Core Output Parsers.

986
02:08:40,366 --> 02:08:45,668
This is a tool that will help us process and clean the raw output produced by our agent.

987
02:08:46,254 --> 02:08:49,988
It ensures that the output is in a readable and consistent format.

988
02:08:50,478 --> 02:08:56,036
First, we created an instance of the STR output parser, which is called parser.

989
02:08:56,718 --> 02:09:03,620
This instance will be used to parse and clean the agent's responses, transforming them into user-friendly text.

990
02:09:04,398 --> 02:09:08,420
Next is to parse the agent's responses using the output parser.

991
02:09:09,006 --> 02:09:13,220
So here, these two functions will take both the responses and process it.

992
02:09:13,838 --> 02:09:19,408
Then the output parser ensures the results are clean, readable, and ready for display.

993
02:09:19,662 --> 02:09:23,556
Finally, we print the clean formatted responses for both the tasks.

994
02:09:24,590 --> 02:09:33,732
So here, parse response 1 shows the capitalized version of the sentence, and parse response 2 shows the reverse function of the sentence.

995
02:09:34,670 --> 02:09:41,764
The output parser ensures the results are displayed in a readable and clean format, making them more user-friendly.

996
02:09:42,382 --> 02:09:47,524
It also helps clean up and format the raw output from the agent before displaying it.

997
02:09:48,302 --> 02:09:57,348
This is important when dealing with complex tasks or when the output might contain extra spaces, special characters, or other formatting issues.

998
02:09:58,030 --> 02:10:01,732
It ensures that the final output is easy to read and use.

999
02:10:02,606 --> 02:10:08,612
The output we see here is in the clean format and version of the raw output generated by the agent.

1000
02:10:09,326 --> 02:10:10,624
Now let's look at memory.

1001
02:10:10,990 --> 02:10:22,944
In Langchain, memory allows the model to retain context across interactions, which is crucial for creating applications that need to remember past interactions, such as conversation history.

1002
02:10:23,104 --> 02:10:31,584
Next, we built a memory-enabled agent using Langchain's Conversation Buffer Memory and OpenAI's GPT 3.5 turbo model.

1003
02:10:32,302 --> 02:10:43,076
The agent maintains context across multiple interactions, allowing it to remember past messages and provide more intelligent responses based on prior conversations.

1004
02:10:43,662 --> 02:10:52,356
We start by installing Langchain Classic, which includes conversation buffer memory, an important component for maintaining conversation history.

1005
02:10:52,942 --> 02:11:01,220
Then we also install Langchain's OpenAI to interact with OpenAI's language models like GPT 3.5 Turbo.

1006
02:11:01,806 --> 02:11:03,876
Next comes setting your API key.

1007
02:11:04,206 --> 02:11:08,132
The OpenAI API key is stored in the environment variable.

1008
02:11:08,558 --> 02:11:12,452
This key authenticates the request made to OpenAI's API.

1009
02:11:12,974 --> 02:11:17,444
Optionally, if you are using OpenRouter, you can set the base URL.

1010
02:11:17,838 --> 02:11:22,596
This is essential to authenticate and access OpenAI's language model securely.

1011
02:11:22,958 --> 02:11:25,060
Without it, the model won't function.

1012
02:11:25,422 --> 02:11:28,384
Next is creating the language model, LLM.

1013
02:11:29,006 --> 02:11:35,024
The Chat OpenAI object is created, configuring it to use the GPT 3.5 Turbo model.

1014
02:11:35,438 --> 02:11:40,068
The model is initialized with the API key stored in the environment variable.

1015
02:11:41,582 --> 02:11:44,420
Next is initializing conversation history.

1016
02:11:45,038 --> 02:11:51,396
History is a list that stores the sequence of messages exchanged between the user and the AI.

1017
02:11:52,046 --> 02:12:00,964
This history is essential for maintaining the context in conversations, as the model will use this history to generate relevant responses.

1018
02:12:01,966 --> 02:12:05,508
Initially, we add the first user message to the history.

1019
02:12:06,382 --> 02:12:10,308
Storing the conversation history is crucial for maintaining context.

1020
02:12:10,926 --> 02:12:19,044
Without this history, the model would treat each message in isolation, potentially losing coherence in multi-turn conversations.

1021
02:12:20,174 --> 02:12:22,404
Then comes the model invocation.

1022
02:12:22,958 --> 02:12:31,396
The first call to llm.invoke function sends the conversation history, which contains the first user message to the model.

1023
02:12:32,014 --> 02:12:37,252
The AI responds to this initial message, and response is added to the history list.

1024
02:12:38,158 --> 02:12:42,436
Then the model's response is stored in the history as an AI message.

1025
02:12:43,054 --> 02:12:46,372
This allows the agent to remember what it previously said.

1026
02:12:46,830 --> 02:12:51,344
In the second user message, the user asks, Can you remind me what I said?

1027
02:12:51,630 --> 02:12:56,132
So this message is added to the history, continuing the conversation context.

1028
02:12:56,558 --> 02:12:59,460
Then comes model re-invocation with history.

1029
02:12:59,886 --> 02:13:09,636
The model is invoked again, but this time it has access to the full conversation history, which includes both the user's input and AI's previous response.

1030
02:13:10,254 --> 02:13:18,852
This step allows the model to remember previous messages and generate a response based on the entire conversation, not just the current message.

1031
02:13:19,310 --> 02:13:21,060
Finally, the AI response.

1032
02:13:21,646 --> 02:13:27,172
The model provides a response to the user's request based on the entire conversation history.

1033
02:13:27,566 --> 02:13:33,636
This enables the agent to reference past messages, providing more meaningful and coherent responses.

1034
02:13:34,030 --> 02:13:43,716
This approach to memory enables the agent to maintain context, making it capable of handling multi-turn conversations with meaningful back and forth exchanges.

1035
02:13:44,174 --> 02:13:53,476
You can easily extend this by adding more sophisticated memory structures or integrating more advanced features like long-term memory for the complex applications.

1036
02:13:54,062 --> 02:13:55,652
Let's discuss about loaders.

1037
02:13:55,918 --> 02:13:58,020
So loading and processing documents.

1038
02:13:58,958 --> 02:14:08,612
In real world applications, you often need to handle large datasets or documents in various formats, and Langchain makes it easy to work with them.

1039
02:14:09,422 --> 02:14:15,204
We'll walk through loading and processing PDF and CSV files using document loaders.

1040
02:14:16,110 --> 02:14:18,500
Let's start by loading a PDF file.

1041
02:14:18,862 --> 02:14:26,308
In this example, we load a PDF file containing information on sustainable development and extract its contents.

1042
02:14:27,022 --> 02:14:32,100
We begin by installing the necessary libraries and importing PYPDF loader.

1043
02:14:32,750 --> 02:14:38,052
Here, the loader load method loads the PDF and returns the content in documents.

1044
02:14:38,862 --> 02:14:43,812
Here, Len of Docs shows the number of pages or documents in the PDF.

1045
02:14:44,686 --> 02:14:57,060
We display the first 300 characters of the first page's content using Docs page content and the metadata to understand the context of the document like file source and document attributes.

1046
02:14:57,710 --> 02:15:01,712
In the output, you can see that the document contains three pages.

1047
02:15:01,998 --> 02:15:14,212
The first documents text shows the beginning of the content on sustainable development, and the metadata includes detail like the document source, creator, and the total number of pages.

1048
02:15:14,862 --> 02:15:19,332
Now let's move on to loading a CSV file using CSV Loader.

1049
02:15:19,982 --> 02:15:24,388
This is often useful when dealing with structured data in CSV format.

1050
02:15:24,910 --> 02:15:28,964
In this example, we load a CSV file containing order data.

1051
02:15:29,742 --> 02:15:33,764
We use CSV Loader to load the CSV file from a given path.

1052
02:15:34,254 --> 02:15:40,676
Then Lin of Documents outputs the total number of documents or rows loaded from the CSV.

1053
02:15:41,326 --> 02:15:49,572
Then we display the first 150 characters of the document text and its metadata, like source and row number.

1054
02:15:50,222 --> 02:15:56,100
In the output, we can see that 9,800 documents were loaded from the CSV file.

1055
02:15:56,750 --> 02:16:11,684
The first three document snippets shows the first 150 characters of the order data, including details like order ID, order date, and customer name, along with the metadata indicating the source file and row number.

1056
02:16:12,078 --> 02:16:25,152
With PyPDF Loader, Langchain allows us to extract and process content from PDFs, making it easy to convert documents into a structured format that can be fed into a Gen AI model.

1057
02:16:25,998 --> 02:16:37,636
With CSV Loader, Langchain allows you to load CSV files and work with structured data directly, which can be useful for applications requiring data processing or analysis.

1058
02:16:38,190 --> 02:16:39,684
Now let's discuss splitters.

1059
02:16:40,238 --> 02:16:50,596
This is essential when dealing with large document as it ensures that the model can handle text and manageable sizes while maintaining context as much as possible.

1060
02:16:51,086 --> 02:16:58,980
In this example, we use the recursive character text splitter to split a long text into smaller, more digestible pieces.

1061
02:16:59,630 --> 02:17:01,892
Let's take a look at the code and the output.

1062
02:17:02,510 --> 02:17:11,460
The recursive character text splitter splits the last text into chunks of 100 characters with a 20-character overlap between each chunk.

1063
02:17:11,918 --> 02:17:16,388
This ensures that the model can maintain context across adjacent chunks.

1064
02:17:17,070 --> 02:17:24,132
The text is processed into smaller chunks while trying to preserve sentence and paragraph boundaries where possible.

1065
02:17:24,686 --> 02:17:29,412
The length of chunks will show the total number of chunks the text has been divided into.

1066
02:17:29,902 --> 02:17:36,356
Each chunk is printed out, showing how the long text is divided into manageable pieces for processing.

1067
02:17:37,102 --> 02:17:41,828
So in the output, you can see that the long text has been split into three chunks.

1068
02:17:42,606 --> 02:17:45,700
The first chunk contains the beginning of the explanation.

1069
02:17:46,222 --> 02:17:51,236
The second chunk continues the explanation, and the third chunk completes the thought.

1070
02:17:51,854 --> 02:17:53,572
Here is how the chunks are printed.

1071
02:17:54,126 --> 02:18:00,772
The total chunks printed here is three, meaning the original text was split into three manageable parts.

1072
02:18:01,550 --> 02:18:09,892
Each chunk shows a portion of the original text with overlapping characters between adjacent chunks to maintain context and coherence.

1073
02:18:10,574 --> 02:18:22,800
Now that we have seen how splitters work, which is crucial when working with large documents, it is time to move on to the next step, allowing us to divide content efficiently for AI models to process.

1074
02:18:22,960 --> 02:18:26,148
Here we will explore two critical chain architectures.

1075
02:18:26,574 --> 02:18:30,084
One is sequential chains and the other one is custom chains.

1076
02:18:30,702 --> 02:18:40,324
These advanced patterns will enable you to create complex, multi-step workflows and implement specialized business logic tailored to your specific use cases.

1077
02:18:40,878 --> 02:18:41,860
Let's get started.

1078
02:18:42,318 --> 02:18:48,612
Let's begin with sequential chains, which are fundamental building blocks for creating sophisticated AI workflows.

1079
02:18:49,422 --> 02:18:50,916
What is a sequential chain?

1080
02:18:51,534 --> 02:18:59,876
At its core, a sequential chain links multiple LLM calls together, where each output feeds into the next chain in sequence.

1081
02:19:00,366 --> 02:19:02,788
Think of it like an assembly line in a factory.

1082
02:19:03,278 --> 02:19:08,100
Each station performs a specific task and passes the result to the next station.

1083
02:19:08,718 --> 02:19:15,364
Sequential chains break complex tasks into smaller, ordered components that execute step-by-step.

1084
02:19:16,174 --> 02:19:22,960
This modular approach makes it easier to manage, debug, and optimize each part of your workflow independently.

1085
02:19:23,214 --> 02:19:31,172
What makes sequential chains particularly powerful is that each step has its own prompt template and specialized processing logic.

1086
02:19:31,886 --> 02:19:41,060
This means you can tailor each stage to perform its specific function optimally rather than trying to accomplish everything in a single LLM call.

1087
02:19:41,454 --> 02:19:45,444
Now, let's look at some practical use cases of sequential chains.

1088
02:19:45,966 --> 02:19:47,844
First is content generation.

1089
02:19:48,334 --> 02:19:50,916
Imagine you are building a content creation tool.

1090
02:19:51,278 --> 02:19:57,284
Your sequential chain could first create a blog outline, then expand each section with detailed content.

1091
02:19:57,774 --> 02:20:04,740
Next, add SEO metadata for search optimization, and finally, proofread the complete article.

1092
02:20:05,262 --> 02:20:09,828
Each step builds upon the previous one, creating a polished final product.

1093
02:20:10,382 --> 02:20:12,868
Second is data transformation workflows.

1094
02:20:13,326 --> 02:20:15,716
Consider processing multilingual documents.

1095
02:20:16,238 --> 02:20:26,468
Your chain could extract document data, then summarize the key points, translate the summary to another language, and format the output according to specific requirements.

1096
02:20:26,640 --> 02:20:31,940
This sequential approach ensures data flows smoothly through each transformation stage.

1097
02:20:32,846 --> 02:20:35,236
Third is multi-stage analysis.

1098
02:20:35,886 --> 02:20:52,036
For customer feedback analysis, you might analyze feedback to identify common themes, then categorize sentiment for each theme, generate actionable insights from the patterns, and finally create executive summaries for stakeholders.

1099
02:20:52,942 --> 02:20:56,612
Each stage adds a layer of understanding to the raw data.

1100
02:20:57,262 --> 02:20:59,620
Finally, complex reasoning tasks.

1101
02:21:00,462 --> 02:21:14,884
When dealing with mathematical or logical problems, sequential chains can break down problems into manageable steps, solve each component sequentially, and then synthesize the results into a comprehensive final answer.

1102
02:21:15,662 --> 02:21:19,076
This mirrors how humans approach complex problem solving.

1103
02:21:19,502 --> 02:21:24,644
Now let's move on to custom chains, which give you even more control and flexibility.

1104
02:21:25,006 --> 02:21:26,692
What is a custom chain?

1105
02:21:27,374 --> 02:21:37,956
Custom chains are tailored LLM workflows that combine prompts, models, and parsers in unique configurations that you design specifically for your needs.

1106
02:21:38,798 --> 02:21:46,372
While sequential chains follow a linear path, custom chains give you the freedom to implement any logic you can imagine.

1107
02:21:46,862 --> 02:21:52,420
They provide flexibility for custom business logic, conditional branching, and error handling.

1108
02:21:53,102 --> 02:22:00,992
This means your chain can make decisions, take different paths based on conditions, and gracefully handle unexpected situations.

1109
02:22:01,232 --> 02:22:09,060
Most importantly, custom chains offer full control over chain behavior, inputs, outputs, and information flow.

1110
02:22:09,614 --> 02:22:12,100
You are not limited by pre-built structures.

1111
02:22:12,672 --> 02:22:15,460
You are the architect of your AI workflow.

1112
02:22:16,238 --> 02:22:20,228
Unlike agents, it doesn't run an open-ended plan in a loop.

1113
02:22:20,590 --> 02:22:25,572
It follows predefined logic, that is, rules or routing that we design.

1114
02:22:26,190 --> 02:22:28,676
Let's explore the use cases of custom chains.

1115
02:22:29,294 --> 02:22:31,908
First is domain-specific applications.

1116
02:22:32,398 --> 02:22:37,732
You can build specialized chains for legal, medical, or financial use cases.

1117
02:22:38,254 --> 02:22:48,868
For example, a legal document analyzer might need specific validation rules, terminology checking, and compliance verification that are unique to that industry.

1118
02:22:49,486 --> 02:22:53,764
Custom chains let you embed that expertise directly into your workflow.

1119
02:22:54,222 --> 02:22:56,004
Second, dynamic routing.

1120
02:22:56,494 --> 02:23:04,228
Instead of following a fixed path, custom chains can direct inputs to appropriate paths based on content or results.

1121
02:23:04,462 --> 02:23:17,316
Think of a customer service system that routes technical questions to one chain, billing inquiries to another, and general questions to a third, all automatically based on the input analysis.

1122
02:23:19,246 --> 02:23:21,476
Third is external integration.

1123
02:23:22,126 --> 02:23:31,364
Custom chains excel at combining API calls, databases, and third-party services seamlessly with LLM operations.

1124
02:23:32,110 --> 02:23:44,868
You might query a database for customer history, call an external API for real-time pricing, and use an LLM to generate a personalized response, all within a single custom chain.

1125
02:23:46,254 --> 02:23:48,192
Finally, error handling.

1126
02:23:48,878 --> 02:23:51,748
In production systems, things can go wrong.

1127
02:23:52,174 --> 02:23:58,020
Custom chains let you implement fallbacks, validation checks, and automatic corrections.

1128
02:23:58,670 --> 02:24:10,180
If an LLM response doesn't meet quality criteria, your chain can automatically retry with a modified prompt, use a different approach, or escalate to a human reviewer.

1129
02:24:11,214 --> 02:24:17,472
In summary, sequential chains and custom chains are powerful tools in your Langchain toolkit.

1130
02:24:17,646 --> 02:24:28,708
Sequential chains provide elegant solutions for linear, multi-step workflows, while custom chains offer the flexibility to implement any logic your application requires.

1131
02:24:29,518 --> 02:24:37,476
Together, they enable you to build sophisticated, production-ready AI applications that solve real business problems.

1132
02:24:37,966 --> 02:24:51,172
In this module, we'll build a PDF Insights Extractor using lightweight, freely available tools such as PyMU PDF, Sentence Transformers, Phase, and Hugging Face Transformers.

1133
02:24:51,790 --> 02:25:05,668
The goal of this project is to create an intelligent system that reads a PDF document, cleans and understands its content, summarizes key ideas, and answers user questions directly from the document text.

1134
02:25:06,254 --> 02:25:11,588
This project focuses on clarity, reliability, and real-world usability.

1135
02:25:11,950 --> 02:25:21,540
Instead of complex frameworks, we rely on simple, transparent steps so learners clearly understand how document intelligence systems work end to end.

1136
02:25:21,998 --> 02:25:23,588
Let's look at the problem statement.

1137
02:25:24,174 --> 02:25:33,252
Document and professionals work with large numbers of PDF documents, such as reports, policies, research papers, and manuals.

1138
02:25:33,966 --> 02:25:41,060
Manually reading these documents to extract insights or answer questions is time consuming and inefficient.

1139
02:25:41,232 --> 02:25:55,908
The challenge is to build a system that reads PDF documents automatically, understands their content, summarizes important information, and answers user questions accurately using only the document text.

1140
02:25:56,910 --> 02:26:03,364
This mini project solves the problem by turning a static PDF into an interactive knowledge source.

1141
02:26:04,110 --> 02:26:09,648
So, we are building a system that answers questions like what is this document about?

1142
02:26:10,158 --> 02:26:11,460
What are the key ideas?

1143
02:26:12,014 --> 02:26:14,948
How does the document define a specific concept?

1144
02:26:15,726 --> 02:26:18,756
All answers are grounded in the PDF content.

1145
02:26:19,248 --> 02:26:21,508
No external knowledge is injected.

1146
02:26:22,062 --> 02:26:26,768
The key objective includes, first, PDF text extraction.

1147
02:26:26,990 --> 02:26:31,268
This ensures the system works on real business and academic PDFs.

1148
02:26:31,822 --> 02:26:34,660
Next, text cleaning and normalization.

1149
02:26:35,086 --> 02:26:39,300
Clean input improves summarization and question answering quality.

1150
02:26:39,758 --> 02:26:43,108
Then, semantic representation using embeddings.

1151
02:26:43,502 --> 02:26:47,588
This step allows semantic understanding beyond keyword matching.

1152
02:26:48,014 --> 02:26:50,116
Then, document summarization.

1153
02:26:50,446 --> 02:26:53,508
This enables quick understanding of long documents.

1154
02:26:54,030 --> 02:26:56,996
Then question answering over PDF content.

1155
02:26:57,358 --> 02:27:01,348
This transforms static documents into interactive knowledge systems.

1156
02:27:01,998 --> 02:27:07,876
Finally, evaluation and logging captures answers, confidence scores, and quality labels.

1157
02:27:08,334 --> 02:27:10,052
Let's begin with the coding part.

1158
02:27:10,478 --> 02:27:13,892
First, we have installed the required libraries for our project.

1159
02:27:14,190 --> 02:27:22,628
These libraries will help us with PDF parsing, text processing, semantic search, summarization, and question answering.

1160
02:27:22,958 --> 02:27:24,484
Let's discuss one by one.

1161
02:27:24,974 --> 02:27:27,300
PyMU PDF for reading PDFs.

1162
02:27:27,758 --> 02:27:31,172
Then Sentence Transformers is for generating embeddings.

1163
02:27:31,648 --> 02:27:35,908
Then Transformers is for summarization and question answering models.

1164
02:27:36,430 --> 02:27:40,548
And Phi'CPU is for performing fast similarity searches.

1165
02:27:41,038 --> 02:27:43,492
Next is importing required libraries.

1166
02:27:43,982 --> 02:27:54,116
Now that the dependencies are installed, we need to import the required libraries to work with PDFs, embeddings, and models for summarization and question answering.

1167
02:27:54,542 --> 02:27:58,916
Here, RE is regular expression modules used for cleaning the text.

1168
02:27:59,438 --> 02:28:03,940
Then Phi's used for creating the Similarity Search Index for embeddings.

1169
02:28:04,366 --> 02:28:07,268
NumPy is used for handling array operations.

1170
02:28:07,648 --> 02:28:11,088
Then sentence transformers for creating sentence embeddings.

1171
02:28:11,342 --> 02:28:17,796
Pipeline from Transformers is for performing summarization and question answering using pre-trained models.

1172
02:28:18,958 --> 02:28:24,836
With these libraries in place, we will move to load and extract the content from our PDF file.

1173
02:28:25,646 --> 02:28:32,516
Next, we will load the PDF file containing our content on sustainable development and extract its text.

1174
02:28:33,422 --> 02:28:36,580
Here we first set the path to the PDF document.

1175
02:28:37,326 --> 02:28:44,548
Next is LoadPDF function, opens the PDF file and reads the text content of each page.

