1409
02:53:46,512 --> 02:53:50,404
Alright, let's kick things off with an introduction to AI agents.

1410
02:53:51,246 --> 02:54:00,228
AI agents are autonomous systems designed to interact with their environment, make decisions, and take actions to achieve specific goals.

1411
02:54:01,102 --> 02:54:13,412
Imagine a system that doesn't just follow a set of instructions, but instead actively perceives its environment, processes the incoming data, and decides the best course of action on its time.

1412
02:54:14,094 --> 02:54:20,804
It's like having an assistant that adapts via memory and program policies, making context-aware choices.

1413
02:54:21,390 --> 02:54:24,260
Let's look at the key characteristics of AI agents.

1414
02:54:24,942 --> 02:54:26,756
First one is autonomy.

1415
02:54:27,246 --> 02:54:32,100
These agents are designed to operate on their own with minimal human intervention.

1416
02:54:32,686 --> 02:54:38,180
They are capable of gathering input, processing it, and acting without constant guidance.

1417
02:54:38,766 --> 02:54:46,564
However, in LLM agents, this autonomy is bounded by graph policies, guardrails, and interrupts.

1418
02:54:47,214 --> 02:54:56,272
This ensures that while they perform tasks independently, they still operate within set parameters, giving you more time to focus on other things.

1419
02:54:56,512 --> 02:54:58,116
Next is reactivity.

1420
02:54:58,670 --> 02:55:00,548
AI agents are responsive.

1421
02:55:00,942 --> 02:55:05,860
If something changes in their environment, they adapt and adjust their actions accordingly.

1422
02:55:06,478 --> 02:55:08,452
Third one is proactivity.

1423
02:55:08,942 --> 02:55:10,852
These agents don't just react.

1424
02:55:11,214 --> 02:55:14,568
They anticipate needs or problems before they arrive.

1425
02:55:15,214 --> 02:55:23,748
For instance, an AI assistant might schedule a reminder for a meeting based on your email, anticipating the need before you even ask.

1426
02:55:24,174 --> 02:55:25,924
Finally, social ability.

1427
02:55:26,350 --> 02:55:29,872
AI agents can interact with other agents and humans.

1428
02:55:30,126 --> 02:55:35,396
They are not isolated, but are capable of collaborating, which makes them even more powerful.

1429
02:55:36,142 --> 02:55:41,348
It's like having a team of AI agents, all working together to achieve a common goal.

1430
02:55:42,222 --> 02:55:53,444
Each of these characteristics makes AI agents highly effective in dynamic environments, allowing them to perform a wide range of tasks intelligently and efficiently.

1431
02:55:53,998 --> 02:55:59,204
Now let's take a deeper dive into the different types of AI agents you might encounter.

1432
02:55:59,950 --> 02:56:05,444
Understanding these will give you insights into the various approaches to building intelligent systems.

1433
02:56:06,030 --> 02:56:08,420
First, we have the simple reflex agents.

1434
02:56:09,102 --> 02:56:11,972
These agents react only to the current percept.

1435
02:56:12,462 --> 02:56:15,364
They don't maintain any memory of past actions.

1436
02:56:15,822 --> 02:56:18,756
If they see something, they respond, but that's it.

1437
02:56:19,214 --> 02:56:25,156
For example, think of a thermostat that turns the heating on when the temperature drops below a certain point.

1438
02:56:25,710 --> 02:56:27,268
It's purely reactive.

1439
02:56:27,758 --> 02:56:29,732
Next is model-based agents.

1440
02:56:30,446 --> 02:56:34,496
These agents go a step further by having an internal model of the world.

1441
02:56:34,702 --> 02:56:39,652
They remember past events and actions, which helps them make better decisions in the future.

1442
02:56:40,590 --> 02:56:47,428
In classical AIMA model, base reflex agents maintain an internal state to inform decisions.

1443
02:56:48,302 --> 02:56:55,140
Imagine a self-driving car that remembers previous routes and adjusts its behavior based on that experience.

1444
02:56:55,950 --> 02:56:58,116
Third one is goal-based agents.

1445
02:56:58,830 --> 02:57:01,956
These agents act with a specific goal in mind.

1446
02:57:02,510 --> 02:57:05,776
Every action they take is aimed at achieving that goal.

1447
02:57:06,030 --> 02:57:13,988
For example, a navigation app guides you towards your destination, choosing the best path based on traffic and other conditions.

1448
02:57:14,606 --> 02:57:16,996
Fourth, we have utility-based agents.

1449
02:57:17,678 --> 02:57:23,012
These are advanced, goal-based agents that try to maximize a utility function.

1450
02:57:23,758 --> 02:57:31,140
Rather than just achieving a goal, they take multiple factors into account to ensure they get the best possible outcome.

1451
02:57:31,694 --> 02:57:40,836
For example, a personal assistant might optimize your schedule, not just for time, but for comfort, location, and task priority.

1452
02:57:41,262 --> 02:57:45,860
Finally, we have learning-based agents, which can learn from their experiences.

1453
02:57:46,510 --> 02:57:51,876
Over time, these agents improve their performance based on feedback and past actions.

1454
02:57:52,622 --> 02:57:58,244
This is like a recommendation engine that gets better at suggesting movies or products the more you use it.

1455
02:57:58,638 --> 02:58:04,196
These different agent types give us various ways to approach the problem of building intelligent systems.

1456
02:58:04,496 --> 02:58:12,228
As we move forward with Langraph, you'll see how these agents come into play and how Langraph helps create powerful AI agents.

1457
02:58:12,750 --> 02:58:17,412
For an AI agent to function effectively, it needs four core components.

1458
02:58:18,030 --> 02:58:24,740
These are the building blocks that allow agents to perceive their environment, make decisions, and take actions.

1459
02:58:25,294 --> 02:58:27,076
First component is perception.

1460
02:58:27,470 --> 02:58:29,956
This is how the agent senses its environment.

1461
02:58:30,414 --> 02:58:38,436
It could be as simple as receiving text or voice input, or as complex as interpreting images or data from multiple sensors.

1462
02:58:38,830 --> 02:58:44,416
Perception is the agent's window to the world, helping it understand what's happening around it.

1463
02:58:44,576 --> 02:58:46,372
Next is decision making.

1464
02:58:46,958 --> 02:58:52,932
Once an agent perceives its environment, it needs to process that data and decide what action to take.

1465
02:58:53,454 --> 02:59:01,764
This could involve reasoning, logic, or even machine learning algorithms to predict the best possible action based on the situation.

1466
02:59:02,734 --> 02:59:10,212
Once the agent has made its decision, it transitions to the action phase, where it executes the chosen course of action.

1467
02:59:10,766 --> 02:59:19,812
Whether it's sending a command, making a choice, or interacting with a physical object, this step allows the agent to influence its environment.

1468
02:59:20,622 --> 02:59:22,180
And then we have memory.

1469
02:59:22,766 --> 02:59:27,172
To function intelligently, an agent needs to remember past interactions.

1470
02:59:27,822 --> 02:59:34,692
Memory helps the agent learn from its experiences, adapt, and maintain continuity in its actions.

1471
02:59:35,310 --> 02:59:42,820
For instance, a personal assistant that remembers your preferences over time can offer a more personalized experience.

1472
02:59:43,374 --> 02:59:55,012
These four components, perception, decision making, action, and memory, are crucial for building effective AI agents, and they will all come into play as we work with Langgraph.

1473
02:59:55,630 --> 03:00:04,036
Let's now dive into Langgraph, an extension of Langchain that's specifically designed for building stateful, multi-actor applications.

1474
03:00:04,462 --> 03:00:11,748
But before I explain what makes Langgraph special, let's establish some important context by understanding Langchain itself.

1475
03:00:12,430 --> 03:00:14,212
So, what is Langchain?

1476
03:00:14,798 --> 03:00:20,644
Langchain is a framework designed to simplify building applications powered by large language models.

1477
03:00:21,230 --> 03:00:24,100
Think of it as your toolkit for working with LLMs.

1478
03:00:24,288 --> 03:00:33,348
It provides reusable components like prompt templates, chains, and memory systems that connect LLMs with external tools and data sources.

1479
03:00:33,870 --> 03:00:45,604
Langchain truly revolutionized how developers integrate LLMs into production workflows and made it possible to build sophisticated AI applications without reinventing the wheel every time.

1480
03:00:46,030 --> 03:00:53,156
However, and this is important, Langchain operates primarily through linear or sequential training of operations.

1481
03:00:53,870 --> 03:00:59,396
You define a sequence, step one leads to step two, which leads to step three, and so on.

1482
03:01:00,046 --> 03:01:02,756
For many applications, this works perfectly well.

1483
03:01:03,182 --> 03:01:05,668
But what happens when your workflow isn't linear?

1484
03:01:06,094 --> 03:01:11,204
What happens when you need conditional branching, loops, or multiple agents working together?

1485
03:01:11,758 --> 03:01:13,572
That is where Langgraph comes in.

1486
03:01:13,966 --> 03:01:18,436
Now let's talk about Langgraph and how it changes the game for building AI agents.

1487
03:01:19,182 --> 03:01:24,932
Let me walk you through what makes Langgraph truly powerful for building sophisticated AI agents.

1488
03:01:25,358 --> 03:01:28,688
First is to create complex branching conversation flows.

1489
03:01:28,910 --> 03:01:32,772
With Langgraph, your agents do not follow a fixed line of steps.

1490
03:01:33,168 --> 03:01:35,428
Instead, they operate as a graph.

1491
03:01:36,110 --> 03:01:40,932
This means the flow can branch, loop, or fork based on conditions.

1492
03:01:41,742 --> 03:01:52,996
For example, a support agent might take one path for billing queries, another path for technical issues, and a different path if approval or escalation is needed.

1493
03:01:53,550 --> 03:01:57,604
Next, build sophisticated AI agents with state management.

1494
03:01:58,382 --> 03:02:02,528
Lang Graph maintains a shared state across the entire workflow.

1495
03:02:02,734 --> 03:02:10,244
That means your agent remembers what has happened so far, prior messages, decisions, intermediate results.

1496
03:02:10,990 --> 03:02:17,732
This memory makes agents capable of handling long, multi-step interactions where context matters.

1497
03:02:18,414 --> 03:02:27,524
It keeps track of progress, context, and data, enabling meaningful, coherent, sustained conversations or workflows.

1498
03:02:28,110 --> 03:02:31,620
And finally, enables cyclic and conditional workflows.

1499
03:02:32,174 --> 03:02:36,804
Beyond simple branching, Langgraph supports cycles and conditional logic.

1500
03:02:37,262 --> 03:02:47,044
That means workflows can loop back if needed, revisit earlier steps, retry tasks, or change direction based on evolving conditions.

1501
03:02:47,790 --> 03:02:51,236
This ability makes agents robust and flexible.

1502
03:02:51,566 --> 03:02:56,964
Altogether, these capabilities let you build AI agents that are not just scripted boards.

1503
03:02:57,582 --> 03:03:00,388
You get adaptive, context-aware systems.

1504
03:03:00,942 --> 03:03:06,884
You get workflows that handle branching, loops, memory, real-world complexity.

1505
03:03:07,310 --> 03:03:12,676
Now let's explore some key features of LangGraph, which enable building robust AI workflows.

1506
03:03:12,910 --> 03:03:19,332
First, Langraph provides a graph based workflow which visually represents the entire agent logic.

1507
03:03:19,854 --> 03:03:28,932
Unlike linear steps, each node and edge in the graph represents an action or decision point, giving us full transparency over the process.

1508
03:03:29,550 --> 03:03:33,636
You can easily track and modify how the agent behaves at any step.

1509
03:03:34,574 --> 03:03:36,420
Next is conditional routing.

1510
03:03:36,878 --> 03:03:41,796
This feature allows the agent to select different paths dynamically based on conditions.

1511
03:03:42,350 --> 03:03:49,040
For instance, depending on user input or context, the agent can choose one course of action over another.

1512
03:03:49,262 --> 03:03:53,604
It adapts in real time, making decisions that suit the situation.

1513
03:03:54,062 --> 03:03:57,028
Another important aspect is human in the loop.

1514
03:03:57,582 --> 03:04:03,200
Even though the agent can operate autonomously, it allows for human intervention when necessary.

1515
03:04:03,854 --> 03:04:11,364
This is vital for tasks requiring human judgment or for scenarios where the agent needs confirmation before proceeding.

1516
03:04:11,886 --> 03:04:17,636
Langgraph also supports persistence, meaning the agent can save and restore its state.

1517
03:04:18,222 --> 03:04:23,972
So if the system crashes or the process is interrupted, the agent can pick up where it left off.

1518
03:04:24,526 --> 03:04:28,644
This ensures continuity, particularly in long-running workflows.

1519
03:04:29,166 --> 03:04:32,868
Finally, streaming enables real-time response generation.

1520
03:04:33,262 --> 03:04:44,132
In situations where instant feedback is necessary, this feature allows agents to send partial results as they are being produced, rather than waiting for the entire response to be ready.

1521
03:04:44,686 --> 03:04:48,400
This enhances user experience by providing immediate feedback.

1522
03:04:48,686 --> 03:04:58,500
So now that we have covered what LaneGraph lets you do, its core features and capabilities, let's look under the hood and the building blocks that make all of that possible.

1523
03:04:59,118 --> 03:05:01,348
At the core of the graph, we have nodes.

1524
03:05:02,062 --> 03:05:05,764
These are the individual processing steps or agents' actions.

1525
03:05:06,606 --> 03:05:16,452
Each node represents a specific task the agent performs, such as generating a response, querying a database, or executing an external tool.

1526
03:05:16,878 --> 03:05:18,244
Next we have edges.

1527
03:05:18,766 --> 03:05:24,560
These define the connections between nodes, determining the flow of information and execution.

1528
03:05:24,878 --> 03:05:32,480
Edges specify how the agents move from one task to the next, based on conditions or outcomes from the previous node.

1529
03:05:32,942 --> 03:05:38,692
LangGraph also uses state, a shared data structure that is accessible across all nodes.

1530
03:05:39,374 --> 03:05:45,604
This state holds context and allows agents to track the information generated during the execution.

1531
03:05:46,190 --> 03:05:52,676
It helps maintain continuity across steps, making the agent aware of past actions and decisions.

1532
03:05:53,038 --> 03:05:57,572
In addition to nodes and edges, tools are an important part of the workflow.

1533
03:05:58,062 --> 03:06:04,836
These are external functions or services that the agent can call to perform tasks outside its core workflow.

1534
03:06:05,294 --> 03:06:12,484
This could include calling APIs, fetching data, or executing other programs the agent is integrated with.

1535
03:06:12,800 --> 03:06:16,388
Lastly, checkpoints allow the agent to save its progress.

1536
03:06:16,942 --> 03:06:22,500
This is particularly useful for long-running tasks where you may need to stop and resume later.

1537
03:06:22,958 --> 03:06:31,280
Checkpoints preserve the state at a specific point, so if there is an interruption, the agent can resume seamlessly without losing any progress.

1538
