1916
03:49:10,062 --> 03:49:11,844
Let's take a closer look at the code.

1917
03:49:12,174 --> 03:49:17,412
We start by defining a function called toolNode, which takes state as its argument.

1918
03:49:17,902 --> 03:49:25,936
Just like the agent function, this state parameter contains the conversation history and allows us to retrieve the necessary messages.

1919
03:49:26,158 --> 03:49:31,748
The main purpose of this function is to execute any tool calls that the agent has decided to make.

1920
03:49:32,654 --> 03:49:36,676
We begin by extracting the last message from the conversation history.

1921
03:49:37,422 --> 03:49:41,444
Next, we look for tool calls in the last message using toolcalls.

1922
03:49:42,222 --> 03:49:45,892
This variable stores a list of all the tool calls that were made.

1923
03:49:46,702 --> 03:49:50,820
We'll also prepare an empty list named results to store our output.

1924
03:49:51,438 --> 03:49:53,956
Now we'll iterate through each tool call.

1925
03:49:54,318 --> 03:49:58,980
For every call, we invoke the search tool and pass the required arguments.

1926
03:49:59,790 --> 03:50:06,244
The invoke method executes the tool and returns the result, which we then append to our results list.

1927
03:50:07,214 --> 03:50:15,396
Each entry includes the role tool, the content from the tool result, and the ID of the tool call for tracking purpose.

1928
03:50:16,078 --> 03:50:19,972
And then we return the results wrapped in a dictionary format.

1929
03:50:21,070 --> 03:50:29,444
In the next step, we'll define the conditional logic that determines whether the agent should continue using tools or end the conversation.

1930
03:50:30,158 --> 03:50:39,396
Should continue functions purpose is to analyze the last message and decide if we should transition to the tool node or end the conversation.

1931
03:50:40,206 --> 03:50:46,276
We then use has underscore added to check for the presence of tool calls in the last message.

1932
03:50:47,022 --> 03:50:50,852
If there are tool calls made, we'll continue to the tool node.

1933
03:50:51,342 --> 03:50:57,828
If no tool calls are present, we'll simply return end, signaling that the workflow should conclude.

1934
03:50:58,670 --> 03:51:05,668
With these components in place, our AI agent is now well equipped to process user requests dynamically.

1935
03:51:06,286 --> 03:51:11,280
In our next step, we'll construct the flow of our AI agent by building the graph.

1936
03:51:11,440 --> 03:51:17,316
This graph will define how our agent interacts with users and makes decisions based on the conversations.

1937
03:51:17,998 --> 03:51:20,004
Let's break down each part of the code.

1938
03:51:20,430 --> 03:51:25,508
We start by creating an instance of state graph, passing in the state we defined earlier.

1939
03:51:26,126 --> 03:51:29,924
This instance will serve as the foundation for our agent's workflow.

1940
03:51:30,350 --> 03:51:32,324
Next, we'll add nodes to our graph.

1941
03:51:32,878 --> 03:51:36,388
Each node represents a specific functionality with our agent.

1942
03:51:36,814 --> 03:51:42,628
In this case, we are adding two nodes, one of the agent and another for invoking the tool node.

1943
03:51:43,310 --> 03:51:49,604
The first node, labeled agent, links to our agent function, which processes user messages.

1944
03:51:50,478 --> 03:51:57,316
The second node, labeled Tools, connects to our ToolNode function, which handles tool executions.

1945
03:51:58,222 --> 03:52:04,676
Now we need to define how these nodes are connected, which we accomplish by adding edges to the graph.

1946
03:52:05,646 --> 03:52:10,148
We start the graph with an edge that connects the start point to the agent node.

1947
03:52:10,862 --> 03:52:18,084
This means when the conversation begins, it will immediately process any user input at the agent's node.

1948
03:52:18,990 --> 03:52:22,340
We then establish conditional edges from the agent node.

1949
03:52:22,990 --> 03:52:30,020
This allows our agent to determine its next step based on the decision process in the Should continue function.

1950
03:52:30,926 --> 03:52:36,068
Finally, we create an edge that loops back from the Tools node to the agent node.

1951
03:52:36,750 --> 03:52:45,764
This ensures that after executing any tool actions, the agent can respond again, maintaining a continuous dialogue with the user.

1952
03:52:46,318 --> 03:52:52,960
Now that we have built our entire graph structure, it's time to compile it into an executable application.

1953
03:52:53,294 --> 03:53:00,228
By calling workflow.compile, we transform our defined graph into an application that can be executed.

1954
03:53:00,942 --> 03:53:08,516
This compiled application integrates all the nodes and edges we have defined, allowing the agent to function effectively.

1955
03:53:09,486 --> 03:53:15,812
The run underscore agent function serves as the interface for users to interact with our AI agent.

1956
03:53:16,558 --> 03:53:22,916
It captures user input, invokes the agent to get a response, and displays the results clearly.

1957
03:53:23,566 --> 03:53:33,092
The first thing we do inside the function is to print a separator for clarity, making it easier to differentiate between user input and responses in our output.

1958
03:53:33,998 --> 03:53:40,100
Next, we pass user's input to our compiled application to get a response from the agent.

1959
03:53:40,878 --> 03:53:47,460
We use the invoke method of our app object, providing it with a message structured in the expected format.

1960
03:53:48,302 --> 03:53:53,988
Here, the message contains the role user and the user's actual input as the content.

1961
03:53:54,958 --> 03:53:59,152
After invoking the agent, we need to extract the response from the result.

1962
03:53:59,822 --> 03:54:06,596
We access the last entry in the results messages and store its content in the variable Final Response.

1963
03:54:07,374 --> 03:54:10,084
This content will contain the agent's reply.

1964
03:54:10,830 --> 03:54:16,132
To complete the interaction, we print the agent's response again with formatting for clarity.

1965
03:54:16,750 --> 03:54:22,244
In this final step, we'll simulate interaction with our agent by running a specific query.

1966
03:54:22,894 --> 03:54:26,308
This will illustrate how the entire setup works in practice.

1967
03:54:26,990 --> 03:54:31,392
In this example, we ask the agent what are the latest developments in AI?

1968
03:54:31,632 --> 03:54:41,252
This question is particularly interesting because it requires the agent to utilize its web search capabilities to provide an informed and up-to-date response.

1969
03:54:41,998 --> 03:54:50,884
Now you have seen how to build a complete AI agent using Langgraph with tool integration, state management, and dynamic workflows.

1970
03:54:51,342 --> 03:55:02,308
To learn how the agent perceives input, decides whether to respond or call tools, executes tasks accordingly, and keeps conversation context across steps.

1971
03:55:02,830 --> 03:55:11,524
With this foundation, your agent is ready to handle real user queries, provide up-to-date information, and adapt over time.

1972
03:55:12,430 --> 03:55:19,428
This practical session shows you how to combine powerful tools to create intelligent, task-oriented agents.

1973
03:55:20,238 --> 03:55:30,788
We are going to dive deep into building a powerful AI customer support agent using some fantastic modern tools like LangGraph and the Grok API.

1974
03:55:31,598 --> 03:55:33,444
Let's move to the problem statement.

1975
03:55:34,062 --> 03:55:36,676
Imagine a typical e-commerce company.

1976
03:55:37,198 --> 03:55:41,152
Customers constantly have questions like, where is my order?

1977
03:55:41,486 --> 03:55:45,732
Tell me about Product X, or, How do I return something?

1978
03:55:46,254 --> 03:55:48,548
These are common, repetitive queries.

1979
03:55:49,102 --> 03:55:52,036
While important, they can tie up human support agents.

1980
03:55:52,590 --> 03:56:04,132
Our goal is to automate these interactions, making customer service faster, more efficient, and available 24 by 7, while still providing accurate and helpful information.

1981
03:56:04,750 --> 03:56:07,108
We'll be leveraging two main frameworks here.

1982
03:56:07,534 --> 03:56:13,124
Langchain, which is a framework designed for developing applications powered by large language models.

1983
03:56:13,806 --> 03:56:17,392
We'll use its core components for defining tools and messages.

1984
03:56:17,678 --> 03:56:20,388
Then Langgraph, built on top of Langchain.

1985
03:56:21,166 --> 03:56:27,492
Langgraph is specifically for building robust, stateful, multi-actor applications with LLMs.

1986
03:56:28,302 --> 03:56:35,204
Think of it as a way to orchestrate complex chains of actions and decisions, much like a finite state machine.

1987
03:56:35,982 --> 03:56:41,924
And for the brain of our agent, the large language model itself, we are using the Grok API.

1988
03:56:42,382 --> 03:56:51,152
Now, a fantastic aspect of Grok is that it provides incredibly fast inference speeds, often much quicker than other providers.

1989
03:56:51,312 --> 03:57:02,212
Plus, at the time of recording, Grok offers a generous free tier for developers, making it a perfect accessible choice for getting started with LLM-powered applications.

1990
03:57:02,606 --> 03:57:04,672
Of course, you're not limited to Grok.

1991
03:57:05,232 --> 03:57:07,684
This architecture is highly flexible.

1992
03:57:08,078 --> 03:57:21,508
You could easily swap out the Grok LLM for other popular providers like OpenAI's GPT models, Anthropic's Cloud, Google's Gemini, or even various open source models that you might host yourself.

1993
03:57:21,870 --> 03:57:23,492
The principles remain the same.

1994
03:57:23,950 --> 03:57:25,232
Let's walk through the code.

1995
03:57:25,550 --> 03:57:33,312
I have prepared a single executable script for a Jupyter Notebook environment, so you can copy and run this entire cell directly.

1996
03:57:33,582 --> 03:57:36,644
First things first, we need our foundational libraries.

1997
03:57:37,198 --> 03:57:49,636
The PIP install command currently installs LangchainGrok for interacting with the Grok API, Langchain Core for the fundamental building blocks, and Langgraph for orchestrating our agent's workflows.

1998
03:57:50,158 --> 03:57:52,272
These are the backbone of our application.

1999
03:57:52,512 --> 03:58:00,676
To begin building our AI customer support agent, we first need to install the requisite packages and then import the necessary modules.

2000
03:58:01,262 --> 03:58:02,852
Let's start with the installation.

2001
03:58:03,438 --> 03:58:11,812
We'll be using the Langchain and Langgraph libraries, which provide the tools necessary for implementing conversational AI technology.

2002
03:58:12,206 --> 03:58:18,884
Now that we have our packages installed, we can proceed to import the necessary modules and components to set up our project.

2003
03:58:19,534 --> 03:58:21,892
Let's break down each import one by one.

2004
03:58:22,350 --> 03:58:24,164
First is OS Library.

2005
03:58:24,622 --> 03:58:28,996
This standard Python library is used for interacting with the operating system.

2006
03:58:29,742 --> 03:58:37,380
It allows us to manage environment variables and path operations, which are essential for handling configurations in our program.

2007
03:58:37,870 --> 03:58:40,420
Type dict, annotated, and sequence.

2008
03:58:41,294 --> 03:58:43,588
These are utilities from the typing module.

2009
03:58:44,110 --> 03:58:48,100
TypeDict allows us to create dictionaries with a specific structure.

2010
03:58:48,750 --> 03:58:58,384
Annotated is useful for adding metadata to type hints, and sequence represents a generic sequence type, which we will use to manage lists of messages.

2011
03:58:58,894 --> 03:59:05,124
SeekModule provides a set of efficient functions corresponding to basic operations on Python objects.

2012
03:59:05,934 --> 03:59:12,196
We can use it to define custom operations and comparisons in our code, enhancing its flexibility.

2013
03:59:12,750 --> 03:59:17,412
BaseMessage, HumanMessage, AI message, and system message.

2014
03:59:17,998 --> 03:59:24,996
These classes from the Langchain Core messages module are integral for managing messages type in our conversations.

2015
03:59:25,294 --> 03:59:38,084
BaseMessage serves as a foundational class, while human message, iMessage, and SystemMessage extend it to represent messages from users, the AI and system prompts, respectively.

2016
03:59:38,702 --> 03:59:40,064
Next is Tool.

2017
03:59:40,686 --> 03:59:48,516
This utility from the Langchain Core Tools modules allows us to define functions that can be used by our agents as tools.

2018
03:59:48,878 --> 03:59:55,396
It simplifies the integration of external capabilities, which the agent can utilize during interactions.

2019
03:59:55,950 --> 03:59:56,784
ChatGROK.

2020
03:59:57,422 --> 04:00:02,916
This class enables access to the GROK API for leveraging powerful language models.

2021
04:00:03,278 --> 04:00:08,784
It allows our agent to generate responses and interact conversationally with users effectively.

2022
04:00:09,102 --> 04:00:10,660
StateGraph and END.

2023
04:00:11,342 --> 04:00:16,932
These classes from the Langgraph module are used to construct the logic flow of our AI agent.

2024
04:00:17,966 --> 04:00:26,852
StateGraph creates a directed graph that represents the interaction flow, while END signifies the conclusion of a workflow within the graph.

2025
04:00:27,342 --> 04:00:28,132
ToolNode.

2026
04:00:28,718 --> 04:00:39,172
This pre-built component allows us to integrate external tools in our graph, enabling the agent to perform specific tasks as needed within the configured workflow.

2027
04:00:39,534 --> 04:00:49,028
With all packages installed and necessary modules imported, we are now prepared to proceed to the next stages of building our AI customer support agent.

2028
04:00:49,390 --> 04:00:53,572
After installing, we import all the necessary classes and functions.

2029
04:00:54,062 --> 04:01:02,660
You will notice Type Dict and Annotated from Typing, which are used for defining a clear and robust state for our agent within Langgraph.

2030
04:01:03,278 --> 04:01:07,492
We also import base message types for handling conversational turns.

2031
04:01:08,046 --> 04:01:11,716
Before our agent can answer questions, it needs some information.

2032
04:01:12,238 --> 04:01:17,584
Here we have set up simple Python dictionaries, OrdersDB and ProductsDB.

2033
04:01:17,744 --> 04:01:19,876
These act as our mock databases.

2034
04:01:20,384 --> 04:01:28,996
OrdersDB holds information about specific customer orders, their status, product, tracking, and delivery dates.

2035
04:01:29,504 --> 04:01:37,700
ProductsDB contains details about the products we sell, like name, price, availability, and brief descriptions.

2036
04:01:38,126 --> 04:01:43,876
In a real-world scenario, these would connect to vector databases or external APIs.

2037
04:01:44,366 --> 04:01:50,384
For our demonstration, these mock databases are perfectly fine for simulating real data lookup.

2038
04:01:50,798 --> 04:01:52,516
Next is defining tools.

2039
04:01:52,704 --> 04:01:56,004
This is where our agent gains its superpowers, its tools.

2040
04:01:56,590 --> 04:02:02,788
We define three Python functions, each adorned with the tool decorator from Langchain Core Tools.

2041
04:02:03,502 --> 04:02:16,388
This decorator is crucial because it tells the LLM that these functions are available for it to call, and it also automatically generates a description for the LLM based on our function DocString.

2042
04:02:16,878 --> 04:02:18,308
Now, Track Order.

2043
04:02:18,830 --> 04:02:21,764
This tool is designed to look up an order's status.

2044
04:02:22,190 --> 04:02:28,384
If a customer asks, where is my package with order ID ORD001?

2045
04:02:28,942 --> 04:02:37,092
the LLM will identify the need for this tool, extract ORD001, and pass it to track order.

2046
04:02:37,582 --> 04:02:43,204
It then fetches the relevant details from our order's database and returns a formatted string.

2047
04:02:43,726 --> 04:02:50,788
Similarly, if a customer asks, tell me about the laptop, our LLM will invoke this tool with laptop.

2048
04:02:51,246 --> 04:02:58,436
It then queries our product's database to retrieve and present details like price, availability, and description.

2049
04:02:58,830 --> 04:02:59,940
Process return.

2050
04:03:00,398 --> 04:03:02,116
This is a more complex tool.

2051
04:03:02,478 --> 04:03:05,984
It takes both an order ID and a reason for the return.

2052
04:03:06,286 --> 04:03:10,052
It checks if the order exists and if it's eligible for return.

2053
04:03:10,702 --> 04:03:17,764
If all conditions are met, it simulates processing a return and provides a return ID and instructions.

2054
04:03:18,958 --> 04:03:23,204
Finally, we gather all these tools into a list called Tools.

2055
04:03:23,950 --> 04:03:28,996
This list will be given to our LLM so it knows the capabilities it possesses.

2056
04:03:30,126 --> 04:03:32,228
Next is Get Product Info.

2057
04:03:32,910 --> 04:03:40,996
After Get Product Info, if a customer asks, Tell me about the laptop, our LLM will invoke this tool with laptop.

2058
04:03:41,200 --> 04:03:48,452
It then queries our products database to retrieve and present details like price, availability, and description.

2059
04:03:48,880 --> 04:03:50,020
Process Return.

2060
04:03:50,702 --> 04:03:52,484
This is a more complex tool.

2061
04:03:52,910 --> 04:03:56,708
It takes both an order ID and a reason for the return.

2062
04:03:57,454 --> 04:04:01,060
It checks if the order exists and if it's eligible for return.

2063
04:04:01,806 --> 04:04:08,452
If all conditions are met, it simulates processing a return and provides a return ID and instructions.

2064
04:04:08,878 --> 04:04:12,868
Finally, we gather all these tools into a list called Tools.

2065
04:04:13,454 --> 04:04:18,212
This list will be given to our LLM so it knows all the capabilities it possesses.

2066
04:04:18,574 --> 04:04:22,400
Now we bring in the brain of our operation, the large language model.

2067
04:04:23,118 --> 04:04:30,080
We instantiate ChatGroc, pointing it to the Llama 3.370P versatile model.

2068
04:04:30,862 --> 04:04:36,804
As I mentioned, Grok is fantastic for its speed, and this particular model is a powerful choice.

2069
04:04:37,550 --> 04:04:39,588
We set temperature equal to zero.

2070
04:04:40,078 --> 04:04:43,840
In LLMs, temperature controls the randomness of the output.

2071
04:04:44,014 --> 04:04:53,668
A temperature of zero means the LLM will try to be as deterministic and factual as possible, which is exactly what we want for a customer support agent.

2072
04:04:54,318 --> 04:04:57,604
We don't want it to get creative when providing order details.

2073
04:04:58,414 --> 04:05:01,284
The line bind tools to LLM is crucial.

2074
04:05:01,838 --> 04:05:05,284
This step binds our custom Python tools to the LLM.

2075
04:05:05,806 --> 04:05:12,836
It tells the LLM, hey, when a user asks something that aligns with the purpose of these tools, you can call them.

2076
04:05:13,390 --> 04:05:21,732
This is how the LLM becomes an intelligent orchestrator, deciding when to use track order versus just generating a text response.

2077
04:05:21,934 --> 04:05:28,804
A quick LLM invoke call is just a sanity check to ensure our connection to Grok is working correctly.

2078
04:05:29,230 --> 04:05:31,300
Next is defining agent state.

2079
04:05:31,886 --> 04:05:37,188
The Langgraph operates on the concept of a state that flows through the different parts of our agent.

2080
04:05:37,806 --> 04:05:41,540
We define this state using a type dict called agent state.

2081
04:05:42,190 --> 04:05:42,948
Messages.

2082
04:05:43,374 --> 04:05:49,668
This is a list that will hold our entire conversation history, including human message and iMessage.

2083
04:05:50,286 --> 04:05:56,100
Annotated sequence-based message ensures that new messages are simply appended to the sequence.

2084
04:05:56,654 --> 04:05:58,212
Then, next step.

2085
04:05:58,542 --> 04:06:04,548
This is a string that helps our graph decide which node to visit next after the agent has made a decision.

2086
04:06:04,942 --> 04:06:07,044
Next step is creating graph nodes.

2087
04:06:07,598 --> 04:06:12,068
Our agent's workflow is broken down into nodes within the Lang Graph Framework.

2088
04:06:12,654 --> 04:06:16,164
Each node represents a distinct step or decision point.

2089
04:06:16,654 --> 04:06:18,628
Here you can see System Prompt.

2090
04:06:19,118 --> 04:06:20,644
This is incredibly important.

2091
04:06:21,038 --> 04:06:25,540
It is a set of instructions given to the LLM before every user message.

2092
04:06:25,838 --> 04:06:30,788
It defines the agent's persona, its capabilities, and its behavioral guidelines.

2093
04:06:31,566 --> 04:06:36,068
This prompt guides the LLM's thinking and ensures it acts as desired.

2094
04:06:36,526 --> 04:06:37,476
Call agent.

2095
04:06:37,870 --> 04:06:39,588
This is our main thinking node.

2096
04:06:39,918 --> 04:06:47,588
It takes the current agent state, prepins the system prompt to the conversation history, and then calls our LLM with tools.

2097
04:06:48,206 --> 04:06:56,080
The LLM processes the input and decides should it directly respond to the user or does it need to call one of our defined tools.

2098
04:06:56,240 --> 04:07:06,244
If the LLM decides to call a tool, response tool calls will contain the tool invocation details, and we set next step to tools.

2099
04:07:07,118 --> 04:07:13,856
If the LLM can respond directly, it won't have tool calls, and we set next step to end.

2100
04:07:14,670 --> 04:07:15,460
Tool node.

2101
04:07:15,918 --> 04:07:19,012
This is a convenient pre-built lane graph node.

2102
04:07:19,438 --> 04:07:24,996
Its sole purpose is to execute any tool calls that the call agent node generated.

2103
04:07:25,742 --> 04:07:31,556
It takes the list of tools we defined earlier and handles the actual function execution.

2104
04:07:31,982 --> 04:07:34,052
Next, route after agent.

2105
04:07:34,510 --> 04:07:36,420
This is a simple routing function.

2106
04:07:36,878 --> 04:07:50,820
After the call agent node runs, this function looks at the state value to determine whether the graph should go next, either to the tools node for tool execution, or directly to end if the agent has a final response.

2107
04:07:51,406 --> 04:07:53,124
Next is building the graph.

2108
04:07:53,646 --> 04:07:59,236
Now we connect all these nodes and define the flow of our agent's brain using Langgraph.

2109
04:07:59,886 --> 04:08:02,436
This is where the magic of orchestration happens.

2110
04:08:03,022 --> 04:08:05,476
The line workflow equal to state graph.

2111
04:08:05,696 --> 04:08:10,436
We create an instance of our state graph, telling it what our state object looks like.

2112
04:08:11,214 --> 04:08:15,972
Next, we add our two main nodes to the graph, giving them descriptive names.

2113
04:08:16,878 --> 04:08:23,140
Every conversation begins by entering the agent node where the LLM first processes the user input.

2114
04:08:23,662 --> 04:08:28,964
Next, workflow addConditional Edges defines the flow after the agent node.

2115
04:08:29,806 --> 04:08:37,572
Based on the root after agent function, if the LLM decided to use a tool, the graph transitions to the tools node.

2116
04:08:38,158 --> 04:08:43,012
Otherwise, if the LLM has a direct answer, the conversation ends.

2117
04:08:43,758 --> 04:08:47,136
This is a crucial edge for multi-turn capabilities.

2118
04:08:47,376 --> 04:08:52,868
After the tools node executes a tool, the graph transitions back to the agent node.

2119
04:08:53,358 --> 04:09:02,148
This allows the LLM to receive the result of the tool and then generate a natural language response to the user based on that information.

2120
04:09:02,606 --> 04:09:03,780
It completes the loop.

2121
04:09:04,270 --> 04:09:10,756
Every conversation begins by entering the agent node, where the LLM first processes the user input.

2122
04:09:11,310 --> 04:09:14,084
Next, workflow add conditional edges.

2123
04:09:14,670 --> 04:09:17,348
This defines the flow after the agent node.

2124
04:09:17,870 --> 04:09:25,860
Based on the route after the agent function, if the LLM decided to use a tool, the graph transitions to the tools node.

2125
04:09:26,254 --> 04:09:30,980
Otherwise, if the LLM has a direct answer, the conversation ends.

2126
04:09:31,470 --> 04:09:32,896
Workflow added.

2127
04:09:33,518 --> 04:09:36,580
This is a crucial edge for multi-turn capabilities.

2128
04:09:37,102 --> 04:09:42,468
After the tools node executes a tool, the graph transitions back to the agent node.

2129
04:09:42,894 --> 04:09:51,716
This allows the LLM to receive the results of the tool and then generate a natural language response to the user based on that information.

2130
04:09:52,206 --> 04:09:53,444
It completes the loop.

2131
04:09:53,838 --> 04:09:55,696
Finally, we compile our graph.

2132
04:09:55,856 --> 04:09:59,396
This prepares it for execution, optimizing the workflow.

2133
04:10:00,078 --> 04:10:06,372
So, to make interacting with our agents easy, we have wrapped the graph invocation in a chat function.

2134
04:10:06,862 --> 04:10:15,524
This function takes a user message and optionally a conversation history for multi-turn interactions and appends your message to the history.

2135
04:10:16,334 --> 04:10:23,056
This creates the initial states for the graphs and invokes our compiled app with the state, letting the graph do its work.

2136
04:10:23,216 --> 04:10:30,596
Then it nicely prints out your message and agent's final response, making the conversation clear and readable.

2137
04:10:31,214 --> 04:10:37,060
Before we go interactive, we have some predefined tests to demonstrate our agent's capabilities.

2138
04:10:37,870 --> 04:10:42,436
We'll run four quick conversations to prove the agent works end to end.

2139
04:10:43,086 --> 04:10:48,932
Tracking an order, product information, return request, general question.

2140
04:10:49,646 --> 04:10:55,076
Each test shows the user input, the tool path, and the expected kind of reply.

2141
04:10:55,822 --> 04:10:58,692
Here you can see test one is to track order.

2142
04:10:59,342 --> 04:11:05,508
Here we have given the user input, hi, I want to track my order, and given the order ID.

2143
04:11:06,190 --> 04:11:18,148
Then Agent Path detects tracking intent, calls track order with the order ID, and the expected reply, current status, last checkpoints, estimated delivery date.

2144
04:11:18,606 --> 04:11:20,868
And the test 2 is product info.

2145
04:11:21,358 --> 04:11:23,264
Here we have given user input.

2146
04:11:23,598 --> 04:11:25,264
Can you tell me about the laptop?

2147
04:11:25,454 --> 04:11:40,068
And the agent path detects product info intent, calls get product info with product name laptop, and the expected reply, short spec, price, availability, helpful link if available.

2148
04:11:40,622 --> 04:11:43,236
So here test one is to track order.

2149
04:11:43,950 --> 04:11:50,020
Here we have given the user input as hi, I want to track my order with the order ID.

2150
04:11:50,670 --> 04:11:59,012
So the agent path detects tracking intent, calls track order with the order ID, and have given the expected response.

2151
04:11:59,662 --> 04:12:02,820
Similarly, test two is about product info.

2152
04:12:03,470 --> 04:12:06,148
Next, test 3 is the return request.

2153
04:12:06,862 --> 04:12:13,028
And test 4 is a general question that user has given the input, like, what can you help me with?

2154
04:12:13,806 --> 04:12:18,244
So these tests prove that the agent parses intent from plain language.

2155
04:12:18,510 --> 04:12:20,356
It picks the right tool when needed.

2156
04:12:21,070 --> 04:12:24,388
It stitches the tool results into a clear answer.

2157
04:12:25,070 --> 04:12:28,164
It replies directly when no tool is needed.

2158
04:12:28,718 --> 04:12:34,116
So next step is where you will take the reins and drive our AI agents with your own inputs.

2159
04:12:34,990 --> 04:12:41,604
In this interactive mode, you will use the chat function to your notebook just like we did in the previous test.

2160
04:12:42,254 --> 04:12:43,876
Let me explain how this works.

2161
04:12:44,430 --> 04:12:48,900
First, let's look at some single-turn examples, tracking and order.

2162
04:12:49,390 --> 04:13:01,956
For example, if you type track order with the order ID, the agent will process this input calling the track order function with the order ID that is ORD002.

2163
04:13:02,574 --> 04:13:08,388
You will see a response that provides the status of your order along with the estimated time of arrival.

2164
04:13:08,718 --> 04:13:17,092
Next, if you type chat, tell me about the phone, the agent will call the GetProductInfo function with the product name phone.

2165
04:13:17,518 --> 04:13:23,748
In this case, you will receive a response detailing the key specifications and price of the phone.

2166
04:13:24,174 --> 04:13:25,732
Then return request.

2167
04:13:25,904 --> 04:13:30,084
If you type chat, I want to return order with the order ID.

2168
04:13:30,894 --> 04:13:35,460
The agent will invoke the process return function using the order ID.

2169
04:13:36,302 --> 04:13:42,884
This enables the agent to outline the return steps and provide you with a reference number for tracking your return.

2170
04:13:43,918 --> 04:13:51,108
Lastly, if you type chat, hi, the agent recognizes that no specific tools are needed for this greeting.

2171
04:13:51,758 --> 04:13:58,624
In response, you will receive a friendly greeting along with a helpful menu of options on how it can assist you.

2172
04:13:58,864 --> 04:14:06,756
Best practices for interacting with the agent is that as you interact with the agent, remember you express your needs in plain language.

2173
04:14:07,118 --> 04:14:14,596
Include the order IDs whenever you have them, and feel free to ask follow-up questions while keeping your history intact.

2174
04:14:14,958 --> 04:14:22,020
If you receive a generic reply, you can always ask the agent to check the order status again for more accuracy.

2175
04:14:22,414 --> 04:14:38,944
And there you have it, a complete functional AI customer support agent built from the ground up using Langchain for tool definition, Langgraph for powerful workflow orchestration, and the incredibly fast Grok API for a large language model.

2176
04:14:39,104 --> 04:14:52,868
This architecture provides a robust way to build intelligent agents that can handle complex multi-step tasks by intelligently deciding when to use their internal tools and when to simply respond conversationally.

2177
04:14:53,422 --> 04:14:57,700
In this session, we will discuss multi agent workflows in Langgraph.

2178
04:14:58,144 --> 04:15:08,068
This module is crucial because it demonstrates how multiple agents can collaborate, each specializing in a specific task to achieve a common goal.

2179
04:15:08,974 --> 04:15:14,212
Multi-agent workflows represent one of the most powerful capabilities of Langgraph.

2180
04:15:14,702 --> 04:15:21,476
And honestly, once you see what's possible here, you will never think about AI systems the same way again.

2181
04:15:21,838 --> 04:15:22,948
Let's get started.

2182
04:15:23,566 --> 04:15:28,356
Imagine you need to prepare a detailed market research report for a new product launch.

2183
04:15:29,166 --> 04:15:36,836
Doing everything yourself means switching between collecting data, analyzing trends, writing reports, and checking quality.

2184
04:15:37,518 --> 04:15:39,556
You lose focus and waste time.

2185
04:15:40,366 --> 04:15:41,988
Now imagine you have a team.

2186
04:15:42,446 --> 04:15:45,796
One person gathers data, another analyzes it.

2187
04:15:46,254 --> 04:15:50,404
A writer creates engaging content, a reviewer checks for errors.

2188
04:15:50,830 --> 04:15:53,124
Each person handles one task well.

2189
04:15:53,614 --> 04:15:55,748
The result is faster and better.

2190
04:15:56,238 --> 04:15:59,136
Collaborative agents work the same way in AI.

2191
04:15:59,376 --> 04:16:02,084
Each agent has one job and does it well.

2192
04:16:02,606 --> 04:16:06,148
One agent can focus on web scraping and data collection.

2193
04:16:06,670 --> 04:16:09,860
Another can handle analysis and find insights.

2194
04:16:10,446 --> 04:16:12,676
A third can write clear summaries.

2195
04:16:13,294 --> 04:16:15,492
The key idea here is teamwork.

2196
04:16:15,856 --> 04:16:17,956
All agents share one goal.

2197
04:16:18,254 --> 04:16:23,492
They pass information to each other and adjust their actions based on what others do.

2198
04:16:24,110 --> 04:16:28,388
The research agent can switch to another data source if one fails.

2199
04:16:28,942 --> 04:16:33,188
The analysis agent can ask for more data if the pattern looks strange.

2200
04:16:33,806 --> 04:16:37,636
The writer agent can update the content as new data comes in.

2201
04:16:38,030 --> 04:16:39,936
They communicate in real time.

2202
04:16:40,096 --> 04:16:43,716
They share tasks and make their own decisions within set limits.

2203
04:16:44,526 --> 04:16:49,508
The flow feels like a smooth, coordinated system where each part knows its role.

2204
04:16:50,254 --> 04:16:53,216
This is what makes collaborative agents so important.

2205
04:16:53,742 --> 04:16:59,620
They combine specialized skills, work together towards one goal, and adapt on their own.

2206
04:17:00,142 --> 04:17:03,364
Deliver stronger, faster, and smarter results.

2207
04:17:03,950 --> 04:17:08,324
Now let's dive into the different ways you can structure these collaborative relationships.

2208
04:17:08,846 --> 04:17:14,724
I have worked with all these patterns and each has its sweet spot depending on what you are trying to achieve.

2209
04:17:15,246 --> 04:17:16,868
The hierarchical structure.

2210
04:17:17,358 --> 04:17:24,932
When you need clear leadership, the hierarchical structure is like a well-run company with clear reporting lines and accountability.

2211
04:17:25,486 --> 04:17:27,972
At the top, you have the supervisor agent.

2212
04:17:28,462 --> 04:17:31,268
Think of this as your project manager extraordinaire.

2213
04:17:31,726 --> 04:17:42,816
This agent has a bird's eye view of the entire operation and makes high-level decisions about resource allocation, task prioritization, and workflow coordination.

2214
04:17:43,246 --> 04:17:45,056
What makes a good supervisor agent?

2215
04:17:45,216 --> 04:17:52,036
It needs exceptional understanding of each specialist's capabilities, current workload, and performance metrics.

2216
04:17:52,686 --> 04:17:59,556
It's constantly monitoring progress, identifying bottlenecks, and making adjustments to keep everything on track.

2217
04:18:00,078 --> 04:18:07,524
The supervisor doesn't need to be an expert in every domain, but it needs to be an expert in orchestration and optimization.

2218
04:18:08,334 --> 04:18:11,460
Beneath the supervisor, you have your specialist agent.

2219
04:18:11,950 --> 04:18:25,540
These are the domain experts, the researchers who know how to extract insights from complex data sets, the writers who can craft compelling narratives, the analysts who can spot trends that others miss.

2220
04:18:26,574 --> 04:18:33,380
Each specialist agent goes deep rather than broad, becoming incredibly proficient in their specific area.

2221
04:18:34,318 --> 04:18:38,244
Task delegation in this model is sophisticated and strategic.

2222
04:18:39,022 --> 04:18:51,524
The supervisor doesn't just randomly assign tasks, it considers factors like agent availability, current workload, past performance, and even the specific requirements of the task.

2223
04:18:52,014 --> 04:19:01,924
Maybe certain research tasks require agents that are particularly good with technical documentation, while others need agents optimized for social media data.

2224
04:19:02,766 --> 04:19:05,796
A smart supervisor makes these nuanced decisions.

2225
04:19:06,446 --> 04:19:09,060
Quality control happens at multiple levels.

2226
04:19:09,934 --> 04:19:23,780
Specialist agents have their own internal quality checks, but the supervisor provides an additional layer of oversight, ensuring consistency across different agents, outputs, and maintaining overall project standards.

2227
04:19:23,952 --> 04:19:32,612
I've seen this pattern work incredibly well for complex long-running projects where coordination is critical and you need clear accountability.

2228
04:19:33,006 --> 04:19:33,952
The downside?

2229
04:19:34,382 --> 04:19:43,268
The supervisor can become a bottleneck if it's not designed properly, and you lose some of the flexibility that comes with more decentralized approaches.

2230
04:19:43,630 --> 04:19:45,092
The peer-to-peer network.

2231
04:19:45,518 --> 04:19:51,524
When you want maximum flexibility, the peer-to-peer model mirrors how effective teams work in real life.

2232
04:19:52,206 --> 04:19:53,540
There's no single boss.

2233
04:19:53,952 --> 04:19:59,428
Instead, all agents have equal authority and collaborate based on skill and need.

2234
04:20:00,302 --> 04:20:05,188
Agents can start discussions, propose ideas, or ask others for help.

2235
04:20:05,966 --> 04:20:13,092
Agents build consensus by exchanging viewpoints and refining ideas until they reach a better solution together.

2236
04:20:14,094 --> 04:20:23,652
For example, one might suggest a statistical approach while another recommends a machine learning one, and through discussions, they merge both.

2237
04:20:24,046 --> 04:20:26,628
Distributed processing happens naturally.

2238
04:20:27,182 --> 04:20:35,908
Complex tasks get divided across agents based on their skills and availability, workflows to whoever can handle it best.

2239
04:20:36,430 --> 04:20:38,852
Self-organization is another strength;

2240
04:20:39,342 --> 04:20:42,884
roles shift automatically depending on the situation.

2241
04:20:43,374 --> 04:20:47,044
One agent may lead in one case and assist in another.

2242
04:20:47,886 --> 04:20:54,276
This setup fits dynamic environments where goals change often and adaptability matters most.

2243
04:20:54,766 --> 04:21:04,100
It needs strong coordination and conflict resolution systems, but when done right, it creates an agile, self-optimizing network of equals.

2244
04:21:04,622 --> 04:21:06,192
The pipeline architecture.

2245
04:21:06,446 --> 04:21:14,340
When you need predictable, optimized flow, the pipeline model is like a manufacturing assembly line, but for information processing.

2246
04:21:14,798 --> 04:21:21,828
It's incredibly efficient when you have well-defined, repeatable processes, where each step builds on the previous one.

2247
04:21:22,254 --> 04:21:30,244
Predictable processing means each agent has a specific role in the workflow and tasks flow through them in a predetermined order.

2248
04:21:30,798 --> 04:21:36,740
This creates predictability and allows each agent to be highly optimized for their specific phase.

2249
04:21:36,992 --> 04:21:49,668
For example, in a content creation pipeline, you might have a data collection agent, then analysis agent, content generation agent, editing agent, and quality assurance agent.

2250
04:21:50,542 --> 04:21:56,132
Each agent represents a specialized state with deep expertise in their phase of processing.

2251
04:21:56,558 --> 04:22:05,604
The data collection agent might be incredibly sophisticated at handling different types of sources, managing rate limits, and cleaning raw data.

2252
04:22:05,902 --> 04:22:11,044
The analysis agent might excel at pattern recognition and statistical analysis.

2253
04:22:11,406 --> 04:22:19,428
The content generation agent might be optimized for creating engaging, readable content that follows specific style guidelines.

2254
04:22:19,886 --> 04:22:27,428
Data transformation happens at each stage, with each agent adding value and refining the output before passing it along.

2255
04:22:27,854 --> 04:22:36,804
This progressive refinement often results in higher quality final outputs because each agent can focus entirely on their area of expertise.

2256
04:22:37,390 --> 04:22:43,792
Quality gates are checkpoints between stages that ensure outputs meet required standards before moving forward.

2257
04:22:44,014 --> 04:22:48,740
This prevents errors from propagating through the system and maintains overall quality.

2258
04:22:49,966 --> 04:22:58,596
If an agent's output doesn't meet the quality threshold, it can be sent back for revision or handled by a specialized error correction process.

2259
04:22:59,150 --> 04:23:03,748
Designing effective multi-agent systems is both an art and a science.

2260
04:23:04,558 --> 04:23:10,340
You need to think carefully about how to divide responsibilities and ensure smooth collaboration.

2261
04:23:11,406 --> 04:23:16,736
Let me walk you through some proven agent specializations that work well in many scenarios.

2262
04:23:17,006 --> 04:23:20,228
Research agent is your information gathering specialist.

2263
04:23:20,782 --> 04:23:28,516
This agent is optimized for web scraping, API interactions, document analysis, and data extraction.

2264
04:23:29,038 --> 04:23:35,972
It knows how to handle different data formats, respect rate limits, and clean and structure raw information.

2265
04:23:36,590 --> 04:23:46,884
A well-designed research agent can navigate complex websites, interpret different document types, and even handle dynamic content that requires interaction.

2266
04:23:47,310 --> 04:23:49,892
The planning agent is your strategic thinker.

2267
04:23:50,382 --> 04:24:01,540
This agent excels at breaking down complex goals into manageable tasks, sequencing actions for optimal efficiency, and adapting plans based on changing conditions.

2268
04:24:02,158 --> 04:24:07,460
It understands dependencies between tasks and can optimize resource allocation.

2269
04:24:07,982 --> 04:24:16,260
The planning agent often works closely with the supervisor in hierarchical models or helps coordinate activities in peer-to-peer networks.

2270
04:24:16,750 --> 04:24:21,216
The execution agent is your doer, the agent that makes things happen.

2271
04:24:21,376 --> 04:24:29,412
It's optimized for taking actions, making API calls, performing file operations, and interacting with external systems.

2272
04:24:30,254 --> 04:24:38,532
This agent needs to be robust with good error handling and retry logic because it's often dealing with the unpredictable outside world.

2273
04:24:39,022 --> 04:24:42,084
Review agent is your quality assurance specialist.

2274
04:24:42,606 --> 04:24:52,100
This agent has sophisticated capabilities for validating outputs, checking for consistency, identifying errors, and suggesting improvements.

2275
04:24:52,558 --> 04:25:01,988
It might use multiple criteria for evaluation: factual accuracy, style consistency, completeness, and alignment with objectives.

2276
04:25:02,176 --> 04:25:04,772
Next is workflow orchestration patterns.

2277
04:25:05,550 --> 04:25:09,376
Now let's talk about how to actually coordinate these agents effectively.

2278
04:25:09,774 --> 04:25:15,940
Supervisorial pipeline works beautifully when you have well-defined processes with clear dependencies.

2279
04:25:16,686 --> 04:25:21,124
Each agent completes their work before passing it to the next agent in line.

2280
04:25:21,710 --> 04:25:30,916
This is perfect for content creation workflows, data processing pipelines, or any scenario where each step builds directly on the previous one.

2281
04:25:31,470 --> 04:25:35,076
The advantage is predictability and ease of debugging.

2282
04:25:35,438 --> 04:25:38,436
If something goes wrong, you know exactly where to look.

2283
04:25:38,958 --> 04:25:41,892
Supervisor model gives you the best of both worlds.

2284
04:25:42,478 --> 04:25:44,976
Next is Langgraph implementation.

2285
04:25:45,422 --> 04:25:51,748
The beauty of Langgraph is how it makes these complex coordination patterns manageable and scalable.

2286
04:25:52,174 --> 04:25:55,204
Let me walk you through the key implementation concepts.

2287
04:25:55,726 --> 04:26:06,176
First, graph-based orchestration means you define your workflows as directed graphs where nodes represent agents and edges define communication paths.

2288
04:26:06,350 --> 04:26:11,652
This visual representation makes it incredibly easy to understand and modify your workflows.

2289
04:26:12,238 --> 04:26:19,140
You can literally see how information flows through your system and identify potential optimization opportunities.

2290
04:26:20,238 --> 04:26:25,252
State driven architecture is crucial for maintaining context across agent interactions.

2291
04:26:26,126 --> 04:26:37,892
Langgraph provides sophisticated state management that allows agents to share information, track progress, and maintain consistency even in complex, long running workflows.

2292
04:26:38,734 --> 04:26:42,208
This shared state acts like the collective memory of your agent.

2293
04:26:42,446 --> 04:26:53,252
Conditional routing logic enables intelligent task routing based on content analysis, agent availability, current system load, and performance metrics.

2294
04:26:53,934 --> 04:26:59,268
The system can automatically adapt to changing conditions without manual intervention.

2295
04:26:59,822 --> 04:27:08,288
Async execution engine allows multiple agents to work in parallel while maintaining state consistency and proper message ordering.

2296
04:27:08,814 --> 04:27:10,564
This is essential for performance.

2297
04:27:10,958 --> 04:27:13,956
You don't want agents sitting idle while others are working.

2298
04:27:14,382 --> 04:27:20,644
Let me discuss with you four compelling examples of how multi-agent systems are making an impact today.

2299
04:27:21,134 --> 04:27:23,236
First, academic research.

2300
04:27:23,694 --> 04:27:28,208
Imagine writing a literature review that requires reading thousands of papers.

2301
04:27:28,462 --> 04:27:49,540
A multi-agent system deploys a paper retrieval agent to find relevant papers, a summarization agent to extract key findings, an analysis agent to identify patterns and research gaps, and a writing agent to synthesize everything into a coherent review, transforming weeks of work into minutes.

2302
04:27:49,966 --> 04:27:55,172
while freeing researchers to focus on original thinking instead of information gathering.

2303
04:27:55,726 --> 04:27:58,020
Second, social media intelligence.

2304
04:27:58,830 --> 04:28:07,620
Brands need to monitor Twitter, Instagram, LinkedIn, and other platforms simultaneously while responding to trends in real time.

2305
04:28:08,366 --> 04:28:11,844
Specialized monitoring agents track each platform.

2306
04:28:12,366 --> 04:28:16,004
A sentiment analysis agent evaluates emotional tone.

2307
04:28:16,398 --> 04:28:19,972
A trend detection agent identifies emerging topics.

2308
04:28:20,526 --> 04:28:24,196
A content generation agent creates appropriate responses.

2309
04:28:24,462 --> 04:28:35,568
And a scheduling agent determines optimal posting times, enabling brands to maintain authentic presence across all platforms without massive teams working 24-7.

2310
04:28:36,206 --> 04:28:38,372
Third, business intelligence.

2311
04:28:38,766 --> 04:28:48,740
When analyzing complex data sets to understand why sales dropped or identify market opportunities, a data cleaning agent handles formatting issues.

2312
04:28:49,134 --> 04:29:13,668
a statistical agent runs calculations, a pattern recognition agent identifies trends, a visualization agent creates insightful charts, a report generation agent writes executive summaries, and a quality assurance agent validates everything, turning weeks of manual analysis into hours of actionable insights with minimal human intervention.

2313
04:29:14,318 --> 04:29:16,288
Fourth, customer service.

2314
04:29:16,494 --> 04:29:26,884
When inquiries vary enormously in complexity and emotion, an intake agent categorizes requests, a sentiment analysis agent, detects, frustration, or urgency.

2315
04:29:27,374 --> 04:29:34,724
a routing agent directs inquiries to specialized agents like technical support, billing, or general information.

2316
04:29:35,182 --> 04:29:45,328
an escalation agent identifies when human intervention is needed, and a follow-up agent ensures satisfaction, getting faster, more accurate responses.

2317
04:29:45,582 --> 04:29:50,596
while human agents can handle only cases requiring genuine empathy and judgment.

2318
04:29:51,438 --> 04:29:58,180
Each agent excels at one task, and together they accomplish what no single system could do efficiently.

2319
04:29:58,638 --> 04:30:07,876
As you learn more, think about repetitive multi-step processes in your field and imagine how specialized agents working together could transform them.

2320
04:30:08,846 --> 04:30:11,568
You've built agents that can plan and take action.

2321
04:30:11,822 --> 04:30:13,924
Now let's give one a real job.

2322
04:30:14,350 --> 04:30:21,764
In this part, your agent works directly with a database so it can answer questions using your own data, not just what it already knows.

2323
04:30:22,414 --> 04:30:30,208
Let's begin our journey by understanding what database agents are and why they are becoming so important in today's data-driven world.

2324
04:30:30,368 --> 04:30:32,848
So, what exactly is a database agent?

2325
04:30:33,294 --> 04:30:40,676
Think of it as your intelligent assistant that can understand what you want from your database and automatically fetch that information for you.

2326
04:30:41,134 --> 04:30:50,852
More technically, a database agent is an intelligent entity that independently interacts with databases to perform tasks such as querying, updating, and managing data.

2327
04:30:51,310 --> 04:30:52,932
But here is what makes it special.

2328
04:30:53,550 --> 04:31:01,508
What makes these agents truly powerful is their ability to employ various tools and external data sources to accomplish specific goals.

2329
04:31:02,094 --> 04:31:15,428
They don't just execute commands, they demonstrate reasoning capabilities to understand what you are asking for, planning abilities to determine the best approach to retrieve or manipulate data, and memory to maintain context throughout interactions.

2330
04:31:16,206 --> 04:31:19,364
Now let's look at the key characteristics that define database agents.

2331
04:31:20,110 --> 04:31:22,084
First is autonomous operation.

2332
04:31:22,638 --> 04:31:25,604
Database agents work independently once you give a task.

2333
04:31:26,030 --> 04:31:30,916
They run multi-step workflows, handle errors, and choose the next action to reach the goal.

2334
04:31:31,502 --> 04:31:35,620
This lets you ask complex questions and rely on the agent to figure out the steps.

2335
04:31:36,334 --> 04:31:39,204
This autonomy works only with strong controls in place.

2336
04:31:39,758 --> 04:31:46,596
In real systems, agents operate inside an orchestrated setup to reduce risks like prompt injection and unsafe tool use.

2337
04:31:47,406 --> 04:31:56,260
They execute multi-step workflows within strict controls, such as read-only credentials, query allow list, and human approval for write operations.

2338
04:31:57,230 --> 04:31:59,556
Second is context-aware decision making.

2339
04:32:00,142 --> 04:32:02,916
These agents understand the context of your request.

2340
04:32:03,150 --> 04:32:11,876
They use prior interactions, database structure, and relationships between data elements to make informed decisions about joins, filters, and query paths.

2341
04:32:12,878 --> 04:32:15,492
Third is integration with various data sources.

2342
04:32:16,142 --> 04:32:19,396
Database agents are not limited to a single database or format.

2343
04:32:19,758 --> 04:32:24,644
They connect to SQL databases, NoSQL systems, CSV files, and APIs.

2344
04:32:25,230 --> 04:32:30,084
This allows them to pull and correlate data across systems to answer complex questions.

2345
04:32:30,670 --> 04:32:35,108
To keep database agents safe in real systems, teams put clear guardrails in place.

2346
04:32:35,758 --> 04:32:38,916
Toolgating ensures the agent only accesses approved tools.

2347
04:32:39,278 --> 04:32:42,372
This prevents unintended actions outside the defined workflow.

2348
04:32:42,894 --> 04:32:47,008
Query allow lists restrict execution to safe pre-approved query patterns.

2349
04:32:47,182 --> 04:32:49,860
This reduces the risk of harmful or unexpected queries.

2350
04:32:50,446 --> 04:32:52,932
Read-only mode is the default for analysis tasks.

2351
04:32:53,518 --> 04:32:57,028
The agent explores data without changing records or schemas.

2352
04:32:57,518 --> 04:33:00,548
Reporting and logging record every query and action.

2353
04:33:01,102 --> 04:33:04,260
This creates traceability and supports review and compliance.

2354
04:33:05,006 --> 04:33:08,836
Human approval is required for destructive actions, like updates or deletes.

2355
04:33:09,518 --> 04:33:13,476
The agent pauses and waits for confirmation before making permanent changes.

2356
04:33:14,158 --> 04:33:17,284
These controls balance autonomy with safety and trust.

2357
04:33:18,030 --> 04:33:23,972
Together, these characteristics explain how database agents work and how they stay safe in real-world systems.

2358
04:33:24,686 --> 04:33:30,404
Let's explore some practical applications where database agents deliver real value across different industries.

2359
04:33:31,150 --> 04:33:33,988
First use case is real-time customer support automation.

2360
04:33:34,670 --> 04:33:43,940
Database agents power intelligent support systems that can instantly access customer records, order histories, and product information to resolve queries without human intervention.

2361
04:33:44,494 --> 04:33:49,732
They retrieve relevant data in real time, providing accurate responses that improve customer satisfaction.

2362
04:33:50,606 --> 04:33:52,676
Second use case is operational monitoring.

2363
04:33:53,294 --> 04:34:01,636
Organizations use database agents to continuously monitor system performance, track key metrics, and identify anomalies in operational data.

2364
04:34:02,126 --> 04:34:09,188
These agents can proactively detect issues by querying databases at regular intervals and alerting teams when thresholds are exceeded.

2365
04:34:10,158 --> 04:34:12,804
Third use case is personalized sales assistance.

2366
04:34:13,390 --> 04:34:20,932
Sales teams benefit for agents that pull customer preferences, purchase history, and interaction data to provide personalized recommendations and insights.

2367
04:34:21,582 --> 04:34:26,468
The agent analyzes patterns and surfaces relevant information exactly when salespeople need it.

2368
04:34:26,990 --> 04:34:30,404
The next use case is financial data analysis and reporting.

2369
04:34:31,022 --> 04:34:38,788
Finance departments leverage database agents to aggregate data from multiple sources, perform complex analysis, and generate reports automatically.

2370
04:34:39,310 --> 04:34:43,588
This accelerates decision making and ensures consistent, accurate financial insights.

2371
04:34:44,462 --> 04:34:48,324
Now, let's understand how these agents actually make working with data easier.

2372
04:34:49,038 --> 04:34:52,356
Natural language querying is perhaps the most exciting feature.

2373
04:34:52,846 --> 04:34:57,700
The most significant benefit is that users can ask questions in plain English instead of writing SQL.

2374
04:34:58,382 --> 04:35:04,612
Show me last quarter's top-performing products becomes a simple conversation rather than a complex query construction task.

2375
04:35:05,198 --> 04:35:14,820
Accurate data retrieval and reporting means database agents can autonomously fetch, analyze, and compile data into reports, reducing manual effort significantly.

2376
04:35:15,662 --> 04:35:22,368
Database agents handle the entire workflow autonomously, without manual intervention, saving hours of repetitive work.

2377
04:35:23,118 --> 04:35:25,412
Integration across diverse data sources.

2378
04:35:25,806 --> 04:35:32,260
Perhaps most importantly, these agents can connect to multiple databases, APIs, and file systems simultaneously.

2379
04:35:32,942 --> 04:35:41,412
They reason across these disparate sources to provide a unified, comprehensive view of your information, breaking down data silos that typically complicate analysis.

2380
04:35:42,062 --> 04:35:46,212
Now, let's understand the core functions of AI in database interactions.

2381
04:35:46,862 --> 04:35:49,348
First is automated query optimization.

2382
04:35:49,774 --> 04:35:54,660
It is mainly handled by the database engine using built-in optimizers and vendor advisors.

2383
04:35:55,246 --> 04:36:00,004
These systems automatically decide execution plans, indexes, and joint strategies.

2384
04:36:00,654 --> 04:36:07,748
AI supports this process by analyzing query patterns and suggesting improvements, like query rewrites or index usage.

2385
04:36:08,334 --> 04:36:13,636
All such suggestions remain advisory and require review and governance before any change is applied.

2386
04:36:14,414 --> 04:36:20,132
Predictive analytics helps identify trends and anomalies in data, aiding in proactive decision making.

2387
04:36:20,654 --> 04:36:25,956
The AI doesn't just tell you what happened, but can predict what might happen next based on historical patterns.

2388
04:36:26,638 --> 04:36:33,732
Conversational language interfaces allow users to interact with databases using conversational language, which we have already discussed.

2389
04:36:34,254 --> 04:36:39,908
But it's worth emphasizing how revolutionary this is for making databases accessible to non-technical users.

2390
04:36:40,654 --> 04:36:45,124
Data quality enhancement focuses on detection first, not blind correction.

2391
04:36:45,710 --> 04:36:53,028
AI helps identify duplicates, missing values, inconsistent formats, and other data quality issues much faster than manual checks.

2392
04:36:53,646 --> 04:36:57,540
The remediation steps rely on predefined rules and domain logic.

2393
04:36:57,966 --> 04:37:01,924
Changes are reviewed by humans before application and recorded for traceability.

2394
04:37:02,478 --> 04:37:06,404
This approach protects data integrity while improving overall data quality.

2395
04:37:06,990 --> 04:37:09,956
Let me share some real-world applications to make this clearer.

2396
04:37:10,542 --> 04:37:16,932
In business intelligence, companies use large-scale analytics and machine learning to extract insights from massive data sets.

2397
04:37:17,582 --> 04:37:23,876
For example, Netflix analyzes viewing patterns across millions of users to guide content strategy and investment decisions.

2398
04:37:24,558 --> 04:37:29,988
These systems rely on recommender models and analytics pipelines, not database agents in the strict sense.

2399
04:37:30,734 --> 04:37:32,548
Database agents serve a different role.

2400
04:37:32,782 --> 04:37:41,700
They translate user intent to safe, governed database operations, such as generating read-only queries, validating filters, or combining data across approved sources.

2401
04:37:42,478 --> 04:37:49,892
The value lies in simplifying how people interact with data, not in replacing large-scale analytics or recommendation systems.

2402
04:37:50,542 --> 04:37:55,556
In healthcare, AI systems support clinicians rather than replace clinical judgment.

2403
04:37:56,014 --> 04:38:03,396
They help by summarizing patient history, retrieving relevant clinical guidelines, and surfacing patterns across large patient data sets.

2404
04:38:03,982 --> 04:38:07,652
This reduces time spent on manual review and information retrieval.

2405
04:38:08,334 --> 04:38:14,372
All insights operate under clinician oversight, with strong privacy controls and regulatory compliance in place.

2406
04:38:14,926 --> 04:38:17,412
Decisions remain with qualified medical professionals.

2407
04:38:18,062 --> 04:38:23,236
In finance, they detect fraudulent activities and predict market trends through data analysis.

2408
04:38:23,886 --> 04:38:30,980
Banks use database agents to analyze transaction patterns in real time to identify potentially fraudulent activities within seconds.

2409
04:38:31,726 --> 04:38:37,668
In e-commerce, they enable personalized customer experiences by analyzing purchasing behaviors and preferences.

2410
04:38:38,414 --> 04:38:48,740
Amazon's recommendation engine is essentially a sophisticated database agent that analyzes your purchase history, browsing behavior, and compares it with similar customers to suggest products you might like.

2411
04:38:49,550 --> 04:38:52,336
Now let's talk about security and safety controls.

2412
04:38:52,654 --> 04:38:56,164
A key security concern in AI systems is prompt injection.

2413
04:38:56,654 --> 04:39:02,564
This happens when malicious instructions are embedded inside user input to manipulate system behavior.

2414
04:39:03,054 --> 04:39:07,844
To address this, the system enforces strict separation between instructions and data.

2415
04:39:08,238 --> 04:39:14,500
System roles remain isolated, and user input is treated only as data, never as executable instructions.

2416
04:39:15,150 --> 04:39:20,036
All inputs are sanitized before processing to remove unsafe patterns or hidden directives.

2417
04:39:20,782 --> 04:39:28,612
Control access is tightly constrained, with the model limited to retrieval operations and no direct access to databases or external APIs.

2418
04:39:29,230 --> 04:39:34,788
After generation, outputs are validated against safety rules, length limits, and content policies.

2419
04:39:35,470 --> 04:39:40,900
A final content filtering layer screens results to block inappropriate or policy-violating content.

2420
04:39:41,454 --> 04:39:47,652
These controls reduce risk, improve predictability, and ensure the system operates safely within defined boundaries.

2421
04:39:48,366 --> 04:39:51,556
Now let's walk through the key limitations in a bit more detail.

2422
04:39:52,014 --> 04:39:53,892
First is hallucination risk.

2423
04:39:54,190 --> 04:39:58,916
At times, the system may generate claims that are not fully grounded in the actual course content.

2424
04:39:59,438 --> 04:40:05,252
This typically happens when the available context is weak or when retrieval does not return strong supporting examples.

2425
04:40:06,062 --> 04:40:08,196
Second is retrieval quality issues.

2426
04:40:08,750 --> 04:40:14,116
Similarly, matching may struggle with new course types or with knowledge-based entries that are outdated.

2427
04:40:14,478 --> 04:40:17,796
When this happens, the output may lose relevance or accuracy.

2428
04:40:18,478 --> 04:40:20,192
Third is ambiguous inputs.

2429
04:40:20,526 --> 04:40:25,380
If the course description is vague or incomplete, the system does not have enough information to work with.

2430
04:40:25,870 --> 04:40:29,284
As a result, the generated copy tends to be safe but generic.

2431
04:40:29,934 --> 04:40:31,524
Fourth are domain challenges.

2432
04:40:32,046 --> 04:40:39,396
Marketing content comes with its own risks, such as overpromising outcomes, drifting away from the brand voice, or missing regulatory requirements.

2433
04:40:40,110 --> 04:40:43,428
These areas need clear guidelines and human oversight.

2434
04:40:44,174 --> 04:40:46,244
Finally, there are system-level error modes.

2435
04:40:46,702 --> 04:40:52,420
API timeouts, token limits, and bias amplification can affect consistency and reliability.

2436
04:40:53,134 --> 04:40:58,692
Together, these limitations explain why guardrails, monitoring, and human review are still essential.

2437
04:40:59,470 --> 04:41:06,084
In this module, you learned what database agents are, how they work, and why they are increasingly important for modern data access.

2438
04:41:06,741 --> 04:41:14,948
who covered their core characteristics, real-world use cases, the role of AI and database interactions, and the guardrails required to keep them safe and reliable.

2439
04:41:15,662 --> 04:41:23,716
